DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nBassoonist/composer joins forces with Olivia Block\, Ben LaMar Gay\, Brian Chase\, and Yarn/Wire to celebrate a new Chaikin release.\n\nIn celebration of\nBIOMES\n\, composer/bassoonist\nKatherine Young’s\nforthcoming Chaikin Records release\, Young performs with electronicist\nOlivia Block\n\, multi-instrumentalist\nBen LaMar Gay\n\, percussionist\nBrian Chase\n\, and the piano-percussion quartet\nYarn/Wire\n.\nThe program features Yarn/Wire performing Young’s immersive\nBIOMES 6.0\n\, a world premiere by Block and Young using tape machines\, and improvisations by Gay\, Chase\, and Young—three distinct voices in creative music.\nKatherine Young\nelectronics\, bassoon\nOlivia Block\nelectronics\nBen LaMar Gay\ncornet\nBrian Chase\npercussion\nLaura Barger\npiano\nJulia Den Boer\npiano\nDustin Donahue\npercussion\nRussell Greenberg\npercussion\n“Young’s compositional hallmarks—her visceral approach to sound\; her organic use of repetition\, structure\, and pacing\; her attentiveness to the smallest details of timbre\; her adventurousness in using instruments in unexpected ways—made the work feel like a living thing\, breathing and unfolding as the evening progressed.” — New Music Box\n“Yarn/Wire may well be the most important new music ensemble on the scene today” — New York Classical Review\n“[Ben LaMar Gay is] hands down\, one of my favorite musicians on the planet today.” — Jeff Parker\n“Block has assembled a deceivingly simple work of utmost subtlety.” — Pitchfork Media\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nKatherine Young\ncreates electro-acoustic music and sonic art as a composer\, installation artist\, and improviser. As a bassoonist\, she amplifies her instrument and employs a flexible electronics setup. Her work has been recognized by the Guggenheim Foundation\, Fromm Foundation\, the Internationales Musikinstitut Darmstadt\, and others.\nOlivia Block\nis a composer and performer based in Chicago who performs live experimental music in festivals and concerts around the world.\nDrummer for the Yeah Yeah Yeahs\,\nBrian Chase\nis an active experimental musician. He owns and operates Chaikin Records\, a label dedicated to the musical language of the avant-garde.\nBen LaMar Gay\nis a composer and cornetist who moves sound\, color\, and space through folkloric filters to produce electro-acoustic collages.\nYarn/Wire\nis a new music quartet dedicated to the promotion of creative\, meaningful live musical experiences in the US and abroad.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260918T000000Z
SUMMARY:Katherine Young: BIOMES (Album Release)
UID:035a42b7-90ff-427d-bf27-081f1b7fdbea@035a.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/biomes-album-release-katherine-young/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nNew quartet explores acoustic-electronic boundaries\, opened with an immersive solo performance of experimental tap dancing.\n\nCecilia Lopez premieres new compositions written for a fresh quartet of exceptional long-time and recent collaborators. Together\, the quartet explores the boundary between acoustic and electronic sound. Through live amplification and processing\, instrumental timbres are altered\, twisted\, and blended into vivid aural hallucinations where individual voices lose themselves in a collective network of sound.\nIngrid Laubrock\nsaxophones\nTrevor Dunn\ndouble bass\nDan Peck\ntuba\nCecilia Lopez\ncomposition\, electronics\, and processing\nOpening the night is\nMelissa Almaguer\nwith a solo set of tap dance and percussion. Her work explores the layered nature of listening and the ways sound shapes our experience of place and time. Beginning with tap dance and expanding into gongs\, seed percussion\, field recordings\, resonant objects\, and electronics\, Almaguer builds evolving sonic environments where movement\, texture\, and rhythm become forms of storytelling through improvisation.\n“One of the most compelling percussionists to hit the New York scene in recent years isn’t found onstage behind a drum kit\, set of congas or any instrument played with sticks or hands: Melissa Almaguer is a tap-dancer\, whose performances recall Whitney Balliett’s characterization of legendary jazz dancer Baby Laurence that he was “more a drummer than a dancer.”\n–\nHank Shteamer JGI New York\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nCecilia Lopez\nis a composer\, musician and multimedia artist from Buenos Aires\, Argentina currently based in New York. She works across the media of performance\, sound\, installation\, and the creation of sound devices and systems. Her work has been performed and exhibited at Museo de Arte Moderno de Buenos Aires (AR)\, Center for Contemporary Arts (Vilnius\, Lithuania)\, Roulette Intermedium\, Issue Project Room\, Ostrava Days Festival 2011 (Ostrava\, Czech Republic)\, MATA Festival 2012\, among others. Grants and awards include a New York State Council for the Arts Individual project grant sponsored by Harvestworks\, the Media Arts Grant (MAAF) for Individual Artists from the New York State Council for the Arts in partnership with Wave Farm\, the Foundation for Contemporary Arts Emergency Grant\, the Jerome Foundation and Roulette Intermedium Commission and the MacCracken Fellowship from the Graduate School of Arts and Sciences at  New York University. In 2019\, Lopez curated the intermedia festival Folly Systems co-produced by Roulette Intermedium and Outpost Artists Resources that featured 11 international artists. Past and current collaborators include Ingrid Laubrock\, Zeena Parkins\, Carmen Baliero\, Aki Onda\, John Driscoll\, Carrie Schneider and Lars Laumann among others. She holds an MFA in Music/Sound from The Milton Avery School of the Arts Bard College and an MA in composition from Wesleyan University. She is currently a PhD Candidate in composition at the GSAS at NYU University.\nIngrid Laubrock\nIngrid Laubrock is an experimental saxophonist and composer interested in exploring the borders between musical realms and creating multi-layered\, dense and often evocative sound worlds. A prolific composer\, Laubrock was named a “true visionary” by pianist and The Kennedy Center’s Artistic Director Jason Moran\, and a“fully committed saxophonist and visionary” by The New Yorker. Her composition\nVogelfreiwas\nnominated “one of the best 25 Classical tracks of 2018” by The New York Times. Laubrock has performed with Anthony Braxton\, Muhal Richard Abrams\, Jason Moran\, Kris Davis\, Nels Cline\, Tyshawn Sorey\, Tomeka Reid\, Mary Halvorson\, Myra Melford\, Zeena Parkins\, Tom Rainey\, Tim Berne\, Dave Douglas\, Wet Ink\, and many others. Laubrock has composed for ensembles ranging from solo to chamber orchestra. Awards include the 2025 German Jazz Prize\, Fellowship in Jazz Composition by the Arts Foundation\, BBC Jazz Prize for Innovation\, SWR German Radio Jazz Prize\, and German Record Critics Quarterly Award. She won the ‘Downbeat Annual Critics Poll”as Rising Star Soprano Saxophonist (2015) and Tenor Saxophonist (2018) as well as Rising Star Composer and Large Ensemble (2025). Ingrid Laubrock is part-time faculty at Columbia University and The New School. She holds an MFA in Music Composition from Vermont College of Fine Arts.\nTrevor Dunn\nwas born in 1968 behind the Redwood Curtain in Eureka\, CA.  His first instrument was the clarinet and he began to focus on the electric bass at 13.  Four years later he co-founded the avant-rock band Mr. Bungle. In 1990\, he received a BA in music from Humboldt State where he performed Koussevitsky’s\nConcerto for Double Bass\nwith the HSO.  In 2001\, he was commissioned by the Elaine Kaufman Cultural Center to write\nDépaysemant\nfor prepared solo contrabass performed later that year at Merkin Hall\, Lincoln Center\, NYC. Dunn has lived in Brooklyn\, NY  since 2000 and can be heard on over 150 recordings including original film music\nFour Films\n(Tzadik)\, his rock band\nMadLove\n(Ipecac)\, and a collection of chamber music\,\nNocturnes\n(Tzadik)\, featuring a string quartet and solo piano pieces.  He can be heard with John Zorn\, Endangered Blood\, Nels Cline Singers\, Tomahawk\, Kris Davis\, Dan Weiss\, and The Melvins with whom in 2012 he toured all 50 States in 50 days. During the pandemic Dunn founded an independent record label Riverworm Records and released his project SpermChurch\, a duo with Dutch-Indonesian electronic musician Sannety.  His third trio-convulsant record\nSèances\n(Pyroclastic)\, amended with a chamber quartet\, was released in 2022.  Other recent releases include more film music\, KingDunn (acoustic duo with Buzz Osborne from the Melvins)\, Ahleuchatistas and Titan To Tachyons.\nDan Peck\nis a tuba player based in NYC. His tuba\, bass\, and percussion trio The Gate\, with Tom Blancarte and Brian Osborne\, has recorded ten albums and toured Europe three times. Earth Tongues is a trumpet\, tuba\, and percussion trio with Joe Moﬀett and Carlo Costa that has recorded five albums and toured domestically. Past collaborators include Anthony Braxton\, Ingrid Laubrock\, Premature Burial (a trio with Peter Evans and Matt Nelson) and Blood of the Stars (duo with violinist Erica Dicker)\, as well as multiple appearances on the Clean Feed\, Firehouse 12 and ugEXPLODE record labels. Dan releases his music and other friends’ stuff on his label Tubapede Records.\nMelissa Almaguer\nis a Mexico-born\, New York City–based multidisciplinary artist whose primary instrument is tap dance. Her practice explores the relationship between movement\, sound\, and perception\, drawing from surrealism\, natural soundscapes\, and the layered textures of everyday listening. Expanding tap through gongs\, drums\, seed percussion\, resonant objects\, and field recordings\, she creates immersive sonic environments where rhythm\, texture\, and movement continuously shape one another. Almaguer has collaborated with artists including Moor Mother\, William Parker\, Joel Ross\, David Virelles\, Patricia Brennan\, and the Jazz at Lincoln Center Orchestra\, as well as tap artists Dormeshia\, Derick K. Grant\, Jason Samuels Smith\, Michela Marino Lerman\, and Jumaane Taylor. A recipient of the 2025 Next Jazz Legacy\, 2024 M³ Mutual Mentorship for Musicians\, and 2023 This Is A Movement: Collective Imagining\, her work has been presented at venues including Lincoln Center\, The Joyce Theater\, National Sawdust\, Joe’s Pub\, and The Jazz Gallery.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261104T010000Z
SUMMARY:Cecilia Lopez’s Filter Four // Melissa Almaguer
UID:565128d8-fdbb-4903-ae09-926e6b354d0a@5651.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/cecilia-lopez-trevor-dunn-dan-peck-ingrid-laubrock/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nA musical journey combining Western jazz\, ambient music\, American folk\, and traditional Indian classical music.\n\nHammered dulcimer virtuoso\nMax ZT\n\,\nsantoor\n(zither) master\nKunal Gunjal\n\, and\nAmit Kavthekar\n\, a top-ranking\ntabla\n(drums) player of his generation\, unite in a groundbreaking and unprecedented musical journey combining Western jazz\, ambient music\, American folk\, and traditional Indian classical music.\nPresented by Robert Browning Associates and Lotus Music & Dance in partnership with Roulette.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nGrammy-nominated\nMax ZT\n\, whose sound draws from deep study with the legendary Shivkumar Sharma in India and the Cissoko griot family in Senegal\, is known for his innovative technique and global approach\; he has recorded extensively beyond genres.\nKunal Gunjal\n\, a Global Music award-winning artist and featured musician on Oscar-winning films\, was a longtime disciple of the great Shivkumar Sharma with whom he performed over the years.\nAmit Kavthekar\nstudied with Alla Rakha and Zakir Hussain\, and has played with many eminent Indian musicians\, including Shivkumar Sharma\, Amjad Ali Khan and Shahid Parvez\, and recently toured with jazz and world music guitarist Al Di Meola.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261017T000000Z
SUMMARY:Max ZT\, Kunal Gunjal\, and Amit Kavthekar: Strings of Soul (RBA)
UID:27e1e992-009f-4862-8dbe-ba4c81bcfd10@27e1.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/max-zt-kunal-gunjal-and-amit-kavthekar-strings-of-soul/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nOne of Zorn’s most challenging masterworks — a complex and varied piano concerto performed by four of his most trusted interpreters.\n\nComposed from 2020 to 2024\,\nAlea Iacta\nEst\n(The Die is Cast)\nis one of Zorn’s most challenging masterworks—a complex and varied piano concerto that runs the gamut of moods\, styles\, and tempi. Performed brilliantly by four of the most trusted and passionate interpreters of his work—the trio of Brian Marsella\, Jorge Roeder\, Ches Smith\, with guest star Sae Hashimoto on vibraphone on one movement—this stunning new work is an essential piece of the Zorn puzzle. Astonishing!\nBrian Marsella\npiano\nJorge Roeder\nbass\nChes Smith\ndrums\nSae Hashimoto\nvibraphone\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260928T000000Z
SUMMARY:John Zorn’s Alea Iacta Est (World Premiere)
UID:2bb6a433-08bf-4e45-b07f-622275633f85@2bb6.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/john-zorns-alea-iacta-est/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $20\nPurchase Tickets\nThe Interpretations Series returns with two ensembles — a percussion\, flute\, sax\, and synth quartet\, and a piano\, percussion\, and bass trio.\n\nThe Interpretations Series continues its 37th season at Roulette with a double bill featuring two ensembles:\nGerry Hemingway\npercussion\nLina Andonovska\nflutes\nEarl Howard\nsaxophone\, synthesizer\n+ Special guest\nKyle Motl\ndouble bass\nEli Wallace\npiano\nNick Neuburg\ndrums\, percussion\nPresented by Interpretations in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nThe Interpretations series\nis a New York-based concert series focusing on the relationship between contemporary composers and their interpreters. Sometimes the interpreters are the composers themselves\; more often\, the series features performers who specialize in the interpretation of new music. Since its inception in 1989\, Interpretations has featured leading figures in contemporary music and multimedia\, including Muhal Richard Abrams\, Robert Ashley\, Anthony Braxton\, Thomas Buckner\, FLUX Quartet\, Joseph Kubera\, Annea Lockwood\, and Alvin Lucier\, Roscoe Mitchell\, Phill Niblock\, Pauline Oliveros\, Ursula Oppens\, and Morton Subotnick.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261204T010000Z
SUMMARY:Interpretations: Gerry Hemingway\, Lina Andonovska\, and Earl Howard // Kyle Motl\, Eli Wallace\, and Nick Neuburg
UID:cc7ea415-fd38-4868-b6dd-2600a6e635ba@cc7e.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/hemingway-andonovska-howard-motl-wallace-neuburg/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $40\nPurchase Tickets\nA two-night showcase curated by Red Hook Records spotlights artists from its catalog and collaborators across its orbit.\n\nRed Hook Records\nmarks its fifth anniversary with two evenings showcasing artists from its roster and collaborators across its orbit.\nSET 1\nAmina Claudine Myers\npiano\nSET 2\nAndrew Cyrille\ndrums\nWadada Leo Smith\ntrumpet\nQasim Naqvi\nmodular synthesizer\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nFounded by producer Sun Chung\,\nRed Hook Records\nlaunched in 2021 with its first release\, the late pianist Masabumi Kikuchi’s\nHanamichi – The Final Studio Recording\n. Since then\, the label has built a distinctive catalog rooted in creative dialogue\, bringing together musicians from different backgrounds and creating new perspectives beyond traditional stylistic boundaries. Its productions have received widespread critical recognition from leading publications across the jazz and audiophile worlds. As The Wire’s Philip Watson observed\, “Red Hook is a label dedicated to exploration\, discovery and pushing boundaries\, to creating something that would not be possible in any other circumstances.”\nAmina Claudine Myers\nis an American pianist\, organist\, vocalist\, and composer whose work bridges jazz\, blues\, gospel\, and avant-garde music. A pioneering member of the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians\, she has spent more than five decades developing a deeply personal musical language rooted in African American traditions while embracing fearless improvisation and innovation. Her collaborations span generations of creative musicians\, and in 2024 she was named an NEA Jazz Master in recognition of her extraordinary contributions to American music. In 2025\, she released Solace of the Mind on Red Hook Records\, a profoundly lyrical solo album that has been widely praised for its intimacy\, spiritual depth\, and masterful artistry\, reaffirming her place as one of the most distinctive voices in contemporary creative music.\nAndrew Cyrille\nis an American drummer\, composer\, and educator whose career has helped shape the course of modern jazz for more than six decades. Renowned for his extraordinary sensitivity\, rhythmic imagination\, and masterful use of space\, he first gained international recognition through his groundbreaking work with Cecil Taylor before collaborating with artists including Wadada Leo Smith\, Bill Frisell\, Marilyn Crispell\, and David Murray. A leading figure in creative improvised music\, Cyrille continues to expand the expressive possibilities of the drum set through performances and recordings that combine technical brilliance with profound musical insight. In 2024\, he released Breaking the Shell on Red Hook Records\, a striking collaboration with Bill Frisell and Kit Downes that explores the unique sonic possibilities of guitar\, pipe organ\, and drums.\nComposer\, trumpeter and author\nWadada Leo Smith\nis one of the creative music world’s most heralded artists. He grew up steeped in the musical traditions of the South\, performing in Delta Blues and other traditional bands before moving to Chicago\, where he joined the legendary AACM collective. Smith defines his work as “Creative Music\,” and his diverse discography reveals a recorded history of music centered in the idea of spiritual harmony and the unification of social and cultural issues of his world. A finalist for the 2013 Pulitzer Prize in Music\, Smith has received numerous other awards and honors including a 2016 Doris Duke Artist Award\, a Guggenheim Fellowship\, the Hammer Museum’s 2016 Mohn Award for Career Achievement\, the UCLA Medal\, and the 2022 Vision Festival’s Lifetime Achievement Award\, among many others. In 2023 he was selected for induction into the American Academy of Arts and Letters. An esteemed educator\, he most recently served as the Spring 2024 Fromm Foundation Visiting Lecturer on Music at Harvard University.\nQasim Naqvi\nis a percussionist\, composer\, and synthesist. In addition to being the drummer of the lauded cult minimalist trio Dawn of Midi\, Naqvi is an accomplished solo artist whose passion for multidisciplinary work has led him into the worlds of film\, dance\, installation art\, and orchestral and chamber music. His collaborators include Wadada Leo Smith\, Andrew Cyrille\, and the BBC Concert Orchestra\, reflecting a practice that seamlessly bridges improvisation and composition. In 2022\, he released Two Centuries on Red Hook Records with Wadada Leo Smith and Andrew Cyrille\, an acclaimed collaboration that explores the intersection of modular synthesis\, trumpet\, and percussion with remarkable lyricism and restraint. The album was awarded the 2023 German Jazz Prize for International Instrumental Album of the Year.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260919T000000Z
SUMMARY:Red Hook Records 5th Anniversary: Andrew Cyrille\, Wadada Leo Smith\, and Qasim Naqvi // Amina Claudine Myers
UID:34c5378e-703d-4286-ba52-ff0fa9328845@34c5.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/red-hook-records-andrew-cyrille-wadada-leo-smith-qasim-naqvi-amina-claudine-myers/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nA one-night\, multi-channel video installation for site-specific performance.\n\nEmanate / Crenellate\nby\nTrouble\n(\nSam Hillmer\nand\nLaura Paris\n) is a one-night\, sculptural and interactive multi-channel video installation for site-specific performance. The environment will be inhabited by a host of esteemed experimentalists\, with the audience roaming free rather than seated. Restructuring habits of assembly has been the thematic driver of Trouble’s two-decade career\;\nEmanate / Crenellate\ncontinues that work into digital space.\nSam Hillmer\nand\nLaura Paris\n(Trouble)\nPatrick Higgins\nDome Theater\nLost Soul Enterprises DJs\n(\nR Gamble\nb2b\nHeidi Sabertooth\n)\n+++ More special guests TBA\n“It was not Trouble’s goal to complicate the performance experience so much as it was to encounter it from it’s own inbuilt interactivity”\n-Warren Fry\, The Brooklyn Rail\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nTrouble\n(2006-present) is the collaborative identity of Sam Hillmer and Laura Paris who live and work in New York City. Their conceptual and polysensory practice spans installation\, sculpture\, performance\, video\, new media and social practice. Trouble’s work focuses on boundaries between social ritual and art.\nIntricate life size maze sculptures are central to Trouble’s practice. Sprawling and complex in nature\, these multimedia maze installations experiment with space and audience experience\, testing the capacity of maze structure to interrupt habits of assembly. Site specific performance programs curated by Trouble are presented inside each of Trouble’s maze installations. Trouble has garnered attention for the visceral audience response to their maze designs: fostering temporary loss of agency by inhibiting audience mobility\, Trouble’s maze work provokes psychic states that hover between ecstatic and exasperated.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261024T000000Z
SUMMARY:Trouble: Emanate / Crenellate
UID:88ee30ba-1e8e-43b3-b094-1c1e92c87b44@88ee.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/trouble-emanate-crenellate-sam-hillmer-laura-paris/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $20\nPurchase Tickets\nA concert film capturing guitarist Miles Okazaki's live performance of the complete works of Thelonious Monk.\n\nJoin us for the world premiere screening of\nThe Monkathon\n\, a concert film documenting\nMiles Okazaki’s\nperformance of the complete works of Thelonious Monk during a five-hour solo concert at The Jazz Gallery in New York City in 2024. This unprecedented event was filmed by director\nFrank Heath\nand transformed into a multi-part film.\nThe evening includes a film screening with one of the four sets from the performance\, interviews with Okazaki\, behind-the-scenes footage\, and an overview of the entire project. The screening will be followed by a Q&A with Okazaki and Heath.\nThe Monkathon\nfilm builds on the recording of the complete Thelonious Monk songbook that Okazaki made in 2017\, during Monk’s centennial year. Upon its release in 2018\, Okazaki’s 6-disc\, 70-track album\nWORK\nreceived wide critical acclaim for its originality and audacity. Nate Chinen described it as “the six-string equivalent of a free solo climb up El Capitan.” The album landed on more than a dozen Top Ten lists for 2018\, and Okazaki was voted the #1 rising star guitarist in the DownBeat Critic’s poll.\n“Utterly contemporary\, free from the expectations of what it means to play a guitar in a group setting — not just in jazz\, but any kind.”  – The New York Times\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nMiles Okazaki\nis a NYC-based guitarist originally from Port Townsend\, a small seaside town in Washington state. His touring and recording experience over three decades includes work with Kenny Barron\, John Zorn\, Steve Coleman\, Miguel Zenón\, Stanley Turrentine\, Henry Threadgill\, Patricia Brennan\, Dan Weiss\, Anthony Tidd\, Adam Rudolph\, Anna Webber\, Matt Mitchell\, Mary Halvorson\, Jonathan Finlayson\, Jen Shyu\, Jacob Garchik\, Jon Irabagon\, Jane Monheit\, Amir ElSaffar\, Darcy James Argue\, and many others. He has released 12 albums of original compositions over the last 12 years on the Sunnyside\, Pi\, and Cygnus labels. In 2018 Okazaki received wide critical acclaim for his six-album recording of the complete compositions of Thelonious Monk for solo guitar\, an unprecedented project that Nate Chinen called “the six-string equivalent of a free solo climb up El Capitan.” In 2024 he recorded the entire songbook again from memory in a one night\, five-hour concert filmed at The Jazz Gallery in NYC. In addition\, Okazaki was voted the #1 rising star guitarist in the Downbeat Magazine critic’s poll\, has published a book Fundamentals of Guitar on Mel Bay\, teaches at Princeton University\, and holds degrees from Harvard University\, Manhattan School of Music\, and the Juilliard School.\nFrank Heath\nwas born in 1982 in St. Joseph\, Missouri. He has exhibited his work in solo shows at the Simone Subal Gallery (New York) and collaboratively at the Swiss Institute (New York)\; Young World (Detroit)\; Simone Subal Gallery (New York)\; Art Basel Statements (Basel)\; and the Videotage Gallery (Hong Kong). His work has exhibited in group shows and screenings at venues including The Kitchen (New York)\; International Film Festival Rotterdam\, The Power Plant (Toronto)\; Centre Pompidou (Paris)\; and the High Line (New York). Heath currently lives and works in New York City.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260923T000000Z
SUMMARY:The Monkathon: Miles Okazaki plays Thelonious Monk (World Premiere Screening)
UID:a75dcdf1-2865-4311-960e-37b94cded9a4@a75d.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/the-monkathon-film-screening-miles-okazaki/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nA two-night series organized around Ronen Givony's recent book\, highlighting the Brooklyn music scene and DIY community of the early aughts.\n\nTo mark the occasion of Ronen Givony’s new book\,\nUs v. Them: The Age of Indie Music and a Decade in New York (2004–2014)\n\,\nRoulette presents a two-night event highlighting the Brooklyn music scene and DIY community of the early aughts.\nNight two features indie rock duo\nThe Fiery Furnaces\n\, along with a rare opening set by Brooklyn group\nSkeletons Big Band\n\, who will perform in an expanded iteration for the first time in a decade. Additional performers to be announced!\nPresented by Wordless Music and BrooklynVegan.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nThe Fiery Furnaces\nare Eleanor and Matthew Friedberger’s rock ‘n’ roll band. A younger sister and an older brother\, once called Pop’s New Prairie School by\nThe New York Times\,\none assumes as a joke\, being natives of Oak Park\, IL\, marooned in and around New York City the better or worse part of three decades\, they write songs\, record songs\, and perform songs in a relatively distinctive manner. Between 2003 and 2009 TFF put out 8 albums of various lengths with various outfits\, a highlight being the quadruple LP\nBitter Tea/Rehearsing My Choir\n(unfortunately not released together). Since the pandemic they have played a number of shows in a number of configurations\, sometimes with two drummers and as many synthesizers as possible\, sometimes with just piano-playing and singing\, and now: guitar\, bass\, and drums. For further information\, ask HAL 9000. Their dad did!\nSkeletons\n(also known as Skeletons and the Girl-Faced Boys\, Skeletons and the Kings of All Cities\, Skeletons Big Band\, and Skeleton$) is an American entertainment unit\, formed in 2002\, in Oberlin\, Ohio. In its various forms\, the band has toured around the world\, and recorded for Ghostly International\, Tomlab\, Crammed Discs\, Altin Village and Mine\, and Shinkoyo — the imprint they co-founded.\nUs v. Them\nThe Age of Indie Music and a Decade in New York (2004-2014)\nIn the tradition of\nJust Kids\nand\nOur Band Could Be Your Life\n\, Ronen Givony’s\nUs v. Them\nchronicles the generation of young artists who came to Brooklyn in the mid-2000s: a small but seismic scene that coalesced under a billionaire mayor\, a series of forever wars\, and a music industry in free fall. Through reporting\, research\, and interviews with musicians\, industry insiders\, and individuals from\nPitchfork\n\, Vice\, Scion\, and the Red Bull Music Academy\,\nUs v. Them\nexamines the rise and fall of indie music in a post-Napster landscape\, marked by vast disruption in technology\, politics\, economics\, journalism\, and patronage.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261003T000000Z
SUMMARY:Us v. Them: The Fiery Furnaces\, Skeletons Big Band\, and Special Guests
UID:14709aa1-41f6-49ff-b73b-0609d88dea84@1470.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/us-v-them-the-fiery-furnaces-skeletons-big-band/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nJazz Generations Initiative Continuum Fellow presents a three night residency featuring poets\, musicians\, and artists across generations.\n\n“Poet Jayne Cortez once spoke the words\, ‘Find your own voice and use it\, use your own voice and find it.’ There is a through-line that connects poetry\, music and visual art … Both are comprised of units of sound\, all have a relationship with rhythm … Tonight we attempt to merge these worlds while still respecting the nuance of each … We are all human\, however each with a signature uniquely our own.” — James Brandon Lewis\nAward-winning saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, a\nJazz Generations Initiative (JGI)\nContinuum Fellow\n\, presents a three-night residency at Roulette as part of his 2026 fellowship. The series brings together improvisers\, poets\, musicians\, and multidisciplinary artists from across media and generations.\nThis evening’s performance features a full ensemble set by\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, along with duo performances by\nAbiodun Oyewole\nand\nTaylor Pace\n\,\nOliver Lake\nand\nJosh Werner\n\, and\nTuesday Taylor\nand\nMelanie Dyer\n.\n“A Saxophonist Who Embodies and Transcends Tradition” — The NY Times\n“James Brandon Lewis does not take the easy road. Having forged a singular sound on the tenor saxophone\, he could simply devise settings that showcase his brawny tone. Instead\, he has rooted his recent music in extramusical research.” — DownBeat\nPresented by the Jazz Generations Initiative in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nThe JGI Continuum Fellowship\nconvenes a cohort of New York-based early-to mid-career musicians and multidisciplinary artists to curate concerts and programs that foster creative exchange across artistic disciplines and generations.\nTenor saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\nhas emerged as a leading figure on the improvised music scene in New York City and across the world. The winner of seven DownBeat magazine awards (including “artist of the year” in the 2025 critics’ poll)\, and a 2026 Guggenheim Fellowship\, Lewis has led ensembles on some of the most prominent stages across the world\, from the Village Vanguard to the Bimhuis Muziekgebouw. He has received accolades from The New York Times\, NPR Music and countless other outlets. The late Sonny Rollins once told Lewis\, “When I listen to you\, I listen to Buddha\, I listen to Confucius… I listen to the deeper meaning of life. You are keeping the world in balance.” Lewis relocated to New York City in 2012\, and since then he has become known as one of the leading figures on the scene\, uniting his background in gospel and jazz with an interest in Western classical music\, hip-hop\, punk rock\, other music traditions from around the globe\, and art forms outside of music. His celebrated projects include the James Brandon Lewis Trio\, the American Book Award-winning Heroes Are Gang Leaders\, the Red Lily Quintet\, and his ongoing collaboration with the Messthetics\, which has yielded two critically acclaimed albums on Impulse! Records. Lewis\, who recently earned a PhD in Creativity from Rowan University in New Jersey\, also works across mediums as a writer and poet.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261010T000000Z
SUMMARY:Find Your Own Voice: James Brandon Lewis // Abiodun Oyewole and Taylor Pace // Oliver Lake and Josh Werner // Tuesday Taylor and Melanie Dyer
UID:2db7501b-ee39-4650-b6c4-e0f2f2175c62@2db7.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/james-brandon-lewis-abiodun-oyweyole-taylor-pace-tuesday-taylor/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nAn interactive sound-movement piece reimagines the Korean folk practice of sangmonori\, followed by the debut of a new improvising trio.\n\nA double bill of improvised electroacoustic music inspired by a reflection on liminal states of being and the evolution of self:\n“To integrate is to submit and self erase\; for some of us\, we have become something else entirely\, having survived and not fully erased. The interstice\, and the in-between state of being. So close yet never home. What is this terrain of the psychogeography we traverse through? Have we come to this land to forget?” – From the performers\nLeo Chang\nopens the night with an interactive sound-movement performance that reimagines the Korean folk practice of sangmonori. The performance features a new iteration of his jeonmonori — a custom-built feedback hat and amplified gong instrument.\nLeo Chang\nelectronics\nAlex Zhang Hungtai\n\,\nTashi Dorji\n\, and\nRaven Chacon\nmake their trio debut with new works. The ensemble merges their distinctive artistic practices for an evening of spontaneous composition\, blending improvisation and expansive sonic exploration.\nTashi Dorji\nguitar\nAlex Zhang Hungtai\nelectronics\, trumpet\nRaven Chacon\nguitar\, electronics\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nRaven Chacon\nis a composer\, performer and installation artist from Albuquerque\, NM. For the past 25 years\, Chacon has been a major contributor to the experimental music communities of the Southwest U.S. In 2022\, Chacon was awarded the Pulitzer Prize in Music for his composition Voiceless Mass\, and in 2023 was named as a MacArthur Fellow. His 2020 Manifest Destiny opera Sweet Land\, co-composed with Du Yun\, received critical acclaim from The LA Times\, The New York Times\, and The New Yorker\, and was named 2021 Opera of the Year by the Music Critics Association of North America. He is in the bands KILT with Bob Bellerue and Sandor Finta\, White People Killed Them with Marshall Trammell and John Dieterich\, the large ensembles Death Convention Singers and Lazyhorse\, and has ongoing duo projects with Laura Ortman and Iggor Cavalera. He currently resides in Hudson Valley\, NY.\nTashi Dorji\nis a Bhutanese guitarist and improvisational musician known for his avant-garde and experimental approach to music. Tashi’s own skewering of guitar traditions has developed an idiosyncratic take on the instrument\, one defined by movement and profound openness to technique.\nAlex Zhang Hungtai\nis a multi-disciplinary artist focused on improvisation and its correlation with the unconscious. After retiring his project Dirty Beaches\, Zhang has been focusing on explorations of improvised music\, free jazz\, and his role as a composer. His newer compositions predominantly work with saxophone and drums\, delving into electronic music. Besides his solo work\, some past collaborators include: Tashi Dorji\, Che Chen\, Chris Williams\, Sam Shalabi\, David Maranha\, and Gabriel Ferrandini. Zhang currently works as a composer for film soundtracks\, along with acting in independent films. He has appeared in David Lynch’s\nTwin Peaks: The Return\nalongside Dean Hurley and Riley Lynch under the fictitious band Trouble. His latest film score\nGodland\nby Hlynur Pálmason was in competition at Cannes Film Festival’s Un Certain Regard. He currently lives in New York City.\nLeo Chang\nis a Korean immigrant experimental musician\, improviser\, composer\, and sound artist based in New York. Informed by his transient lived experience\, Leo plays with sounds\, instruments\, forms\, and social practices outside of their original context. Since 2019\, he has been focused on building electronic performance setups derived from Korean folk practices and instruments. Leo is a 2026-2028 Princeton Arts Fellow\, 2026 NYSCA/NYFA Fellow\, 2023-25 Commissioned and Resident Artist at Roulette Intermedium\, 2024 Center for Performance Research Artist-in-Residence\, and has received grants from the Korea Foundation\, New York Foundation for the Arts\, and Brooklyn Arts Council.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260914T000000Z
SUMMARY:Raven Chacon + Tashi Dorji + Alex Zhang Hungtai // Leo Chang
UID:4587354b-73b2-4b14-a0f8-46ad4e349f6b@4587.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/raven-chacon-tashi-dorji-alex-zhang-hungtai-leo-chang/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $30\nPurchase Tickets\nAn improvised structure for an orchestra of voices\, electric guitars\, and an audience\, using the Bill of Rights as its source material.\n\nIn advance of the midterm elections composer/improviser Carla Kihlstedt and poet Rafael Osés present\nX Amendments\n\,\nan improvised sonic structure for an orchestra of voices\, electric guitars\, and an audience\, u\nsing the first ten amendments of the United States Constitution – The Bill of Rights – as its source material. The performance is an experiment in individual and collective freedoms and an exploration of rights\, responsibilities\, agency\, and restrictions.\nHolland Andrews\nvoice\nGelsey Bell\nvoice\nCarla Kihlstedt\nvoice\nAnaïs Maviel\nvoice\nRafael Osés\nvoice\nDither:\nTaylor Levine\nguitar\nJoshua Lopes\nguitar\nJames Moore\nguitar\nBrendon Randall-Myers\nguitar\nBrandon Ross\nguitar\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nCarla Kihlstedt\nis an American composer\, improviser\, writer and educator who plays violin/viola and sings\, often simultaneously. She loves the economy\, intimacy\, and unapologetic directness of song. In her larger pieces (\nBlack Inscription\, Necessary Monsters\, 26 Little Deaths\, At Night We Walk in Circles and Are Consumed by Fire\,\nPandæmonium\, X Amendments\, 6 Dioramas) she explores the complexity of a single idea through many different lenses.\nCarla is a founding member of the experimental metal band Sleepytime Gorilla Museum\, the composers collective Tin Hat (formerly Tin Hat Trio)\, the song-based projects Rabbit Rabbit Radio and 2 Foot Yard\, and the improvised bands Causing A Tiger\, and Minamo. She is on the faculty of the Contemporary Musical Arts Department of the New England Conservatory\, the MFA in Music Composition program at the Vermont College of Fine Arts at CalArts\, and she co-facilitated the Creative Gesture Lab for composers and choreographers at the Banff Centre for Arts and Creativity. She is currently incubating two new choral projects: Long for This World—an educational\, environmentally-centered youth chorus project\; Feral Choir—an experimental vocal group rooted in the physicality of singing bodies.\nDither\n\, a New York based electric guitar quartet\, is dedicated to an eclectic mix of experimental repertoire which spans composed\, improvised\, and electronic music. Formed in 2007\, the quartet has performed across the United States and abroad\, presenting new commissions\, original compositions\, multimedia works\, and large guitar ensemble pieces. Dither’s members are Taylor Levine\, Joshua Lopes\, James Moore\, and Brendon Randall-Myers.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261009T000000Z
SUMMARY:X AMENDMENTS: Carla Kihlstedt\, Rafael Osés\, and Dither (Night 2)
UID:7658097e-78d1-4186-bbc4-9bbf9dae5eec@7658.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/x-amendments-carla-kihlstedt-rafael-oses-dither-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nFor his Roulette Commission\, Naqvi performs two new works for modular synthesizer and chamber ensemble.\n\nFor his Roulette Commission\,\nQasim Naqvi\nwill perform two new works—a solo study for modular synthesizer and a chamber work for piano\, string trio\, and three improvisers\, featuring\nCaroline Davis\n\,\nGrey Mcmurray\,\nScott Colberg\,\nand members of the\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble.\nA note from Naqvi on the inspiration behind the work:\n“In 2015\, I was invited to create music for a video installation\, and through that project I began to study the music of New York composer Earle Brown and the early work of the Polish composer Krzysztof Penderecki. What drew me to both composers was their unique approach to open form and graphic notational systems—a symbolic notational language that allowed for broad interpretive possibilities within an ensemble. When infused with traditional musical notation and very concise parameters\, the result for me was a kind of magical tightrope between legible and illegible musical form. Like an image distorting and rippling back into its natural state. Through this study\, I created a series of works utilizing a graphic notational system of my own design. This evening’s music is a continuation of that language\, which I am revisiting after almost a decade.\nThese pieces are dedicated to Roulette Intermedium. Creative music institutions have risen and fallen over the decades\, yet Roulette has persevered\, programming some of the most uncompromising and adventurous art in the world. This music is a dedication to their spirit.”\nQasim Naqvi\nmodular synthesizer\nCaroline Davis\nalto saxophone\nGrey Mcmurray\nprepared acoustic guitar\, electric guitar\nScott Colberg\nacoustic bass\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble\nJosh Modney\nviolin\nMiranda Cuckson\nviola\nMichael Nicholas\ncello\nErika Dohi\npiano\n*FREE FOR ALL ROULETTE MEMBERS *\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nQasim Naqvi\nis a percussionist\, composer\, and synthesist. In addition to being the drummer of the lauded cult minimalist trio Dawn of Midi\, Naqvi is an accomplished solo artist whose passion for multidisciplinary work has led him into the worlds of film\, dance\, installation art\, and orchestral and chamber music. His concert music has been commissioned and performed by London Contemporary Orchestra\, BBC Concert Orchestra\, Crash Ensemble\, Bang on a Can All-Stars\, Jennifer Koh\, Stargaze\, Cello Octet Amsterdam\, Helsinki Chamber Choir\, among others. Other collaborations include original music for Academy Award–winning documentarian Laura Poitras and Turner Prize–nominated filmmaker and installation artist Naeem Mohaiemen. Qasim has released music as a bandleader on the labels Erased Tapes Records\, New Amsterdam Records\, Red Hook Records\, and NNA Tapes.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261119T010000Z
SUMMARY:Qasim Naqvi: Slow Television
UID:d364bf9c-4b7c-44ff-9f04-d6bac2bac363@d364.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/qasim-naqvi-slow-television/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Mutual Mentorship for Musicians (M³\n)\, a visionary and non-hierarchical mentorship initiative created by musicians\nJen Shyu\nand\nSara Serpa\n\, presents its\n5\nth annual in-person festival featuring some of the most groundbreaking composer-performers in the jazz\, creative\, electronic\, singer-songwriter\, experimental\, and traditional music scenes\, and beyond.\nM³ Festival 2026 Lineup:\nTerri Lyne Carrington • William Parker •  Patricia Nicholson • Theo Bleckmann • Martha Redbone • Marta Sanchez • Caroline Davis • Aaron Whitby • Alexandria DeWalt • Allan Mednard • Carmen Quill • Carolina Borja-Marroquin • Chanelle Ignant •  Coco Elysses • Devon Gates • Ge Linna • Kweku Aggrey • Kweku Sumbry • Mama Dee • Marcus Grant • Melissa Almaguer • Miranda Agnew • Morgan Guerin • Noa Fort • Paige Brown • Rashaan Carter • Stephan Crump • Summer Kodama • Tomas Fujiwara • Unma Allinar • Vitor Gonçalves • Yuhan Su\nTickets and lineup will be made available via the\nM³ homepage\n.\nRoulette members receive a discount – please email membership@roulette.org for more information.\nPresented by\nM³\nin partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 7pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nMutual Mentorship for Musicians (M³)\nis a non-profit\, 501(c)3 organization and platform created to empower\, elevate\, normalize and give visibility to musicians of historically underrepresented gender identities (including cis women\, trans women\, trans men and non-binary) in intersection with race\, sexuality\, or ability across generations in the US and worldwide\, through a radical model of mentorship and musical collaborative commissions.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261108T000000Z
SUMMARY:M3 Festival 2026
UID:4e99c27c-552f-4058-b445-d9527280d4c6@4e99.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/m3-festival-2026/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nPercussion ensemble joins composers/soloists Brian Chase and John Hollenbeck to premiere two new works.\n\nContemporary percussion ensemble\nTalujon\npresents world premieres by\nBrian Chase\nand\nJohn Hollenbeck\n\, with both composers joining as soloists in their respective works. Each new composition blends the composers’ personal musical languages with Talujon’s disctinct voice and style.\nBrian Chase\ncomposer\, soloist\nJohn Hollenbeck\ncomposer\, soloist\nTalujon\npercussion ensemble:\nIan Antonio\nCaitlin Cawley\nDavid Cossin\nMatthew Gold\nTom Kolor\nMichael Lipsey\nMatt Ward\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nTalujon\nDescribed by the New York Times as possessing an “edgy\, unflagging energy\,” Talujon has committed itself to the growth of contemporary percussion music through diverse performance\, commissioning\, educational\, and outreach activities. Talujon is composed of percussionists Ian Antonio\, Caitlin Cawley\, David Cossin\, Matthew Gold\, Tom Kolor\, Michael Lipsey\, and Matt Ward. Highlights of Talujon’s recent engagements include appearances at the Brooklyn Academy of Music’s Next Wave Festival\, Bang on a Can’s Marathon and Long Play Festivals\, Carnegie Hall\, ISSUE Project Room\, Roulette\, Miller Theatre\, and New York Historical Society. International performances include Taipei’s Lantern Festival and Italy’s Sound Res Festival. Talujon partners with New York City Public Schools and the Midori and Friends organization to produce educational programming across New York City’s five boroughs. The ensemble’s playing can be heard on the Cantaloupe\, Tzadik\, Unseen Worlds\, Another Timbre\, New World\, Bridge\, Albany\, and Capstone record labels.\nBrooklyn-based musician\nBrian Chase\nis the drummer for Grammy-nominated rock band Yeah Yeah Yeahs\, NYC’s experimental music community and Drums and Drones\, a solo project with a compositional focus on the harmonic resonances of drums and percussion. As an exceptionally versatile performer\, Brian’s music has taken him across the world to such notable stages as the Sydney Opera House\, Metropolitan Museum of Art and Coachella as well as countless independent art spaces of music’s ‘underground’ communities. In 2018\, Chase started an independent record label\, Chaikin Records\, which spotlights pioneering figures of the avant-garde. The inaugural release on Chaikin Records was Drums and Drones: Decade\, a triple album with 144 page book\, described in The Wire as “an indispensable statement on how drummers hear sound.” Additional releases by Chase on Chaikin Records include duo albums with harpist Zeena Parkins\, pianist Anthony Coleman\, saxophonist Catherine Sikora and a collaboration with visual artist Keti Katveli. As an educator Chase has taught at Bennington College and was a guest artist at So Percussion’s SoSI festival and Yarn/Wire’s annual summer institute. Chase’s writings have appeared in John Zorn’s Arcana series\, culture blog Talkhouse and drumming trade magazine Modern Drummer.\nJohn Hollenbeck\nis a composer\, drummer\, and percussionist whose work spans jazz\, contemporary classical music\, chamber music\, and large ensemble composition. Since emerging as a bandleader in 2001\, he has led a wide range of acclaimed projects\, including the Claudia Quintet and the John Hollenbeck Large Ensemble\, both recognized for their innovative approaches to ensemble music. Hollenbeck has composed extensively for jazz ensembles\, choirs\, wind ensembles\, chamber groups\, and orchestras\, receiving commissions from major festivals\, universities\, and arts organizations in North America and Europe. His recordings and compositions have earned six GRAMMY nominations\, including nominations for the John Hollenbeck Large Ensemble and his large-ensemble arrangements. A graduate of the Eastman School of Music\, Hollenbeck studied percussion and jazz composition and was mentored by composer and arranger Bob Brookmeyer and composer Meredith Monk. His honors include a Doris Duke Performing Artist Award\, the ASCAP Jazz Vanguard Award\, and a Guggenheim Fellowship. He taught at the Jazz Institute Berlin from 2005–2016 and later joined the faculty of McGill University’s Schulich School of Music. Hollenbeck continues to perform\, compose\, and collaborate internationally as one of today’s leading creative musicians.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261124T010000Z
SUMMARY:Talujon with Brian Chase and John Hollenbeck
UID:11b8ceb2-2526-4082-ab5a-abcbebeb45e8@11b8.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/talujon-brian-chase-john-hollenbeck/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\nComposer and master qanun player marks the release of his new album with a jazz quartet\, hybrid ensemble\, and an Arab takht.\n\nComposer and master qanun player\,\nFiras Zreik\n\, marks the release of his powerful new album\,\nTarabesque\n. He will be joined by\nGideon Forbes\n(sax\, nay-flute)\,\nRamiro Marziani\n(guitar)\,\nJohn Murchison\n(bass)\, and\nAlber Baseel\n(percussion).\nThe album signals the emergence of a new musical language which merges tarab—the ecstatic\, immersive state of listening central to Arab musical traditions—with modern styles from around the world. He places the qanun—the shimmering Mediterranean harp—at the center of a jazz quartet\, a hybrid ensemble\, and an Arab takht ensemble\, reimagining the instrument as both a rhythmic engine and melodic force. With dazzling virtuosity and deep emotional range\, he builds a world of maqam (mode)-driven melodies woven through rich harmonic and modal landscapes that feel defiantly new.\n“The Paco de Lucia of the Qanun” – Washington Report on Middle East Affairs\nPresented by Robert Browning Associates and Lotus Music & Dance in partnership with Roulette and Brooklyn Maqam.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nComposer and master qanun (zither) player\nFiras Zreik\nhas performed and recorded across the world as a leader and highly sought collaborator. The Palestine-born New Yorker has transformed global perception of the qanun. Zreik recently received recognition for his songwriting collaboration with Puerto Rican artist Residente on the song “Bajo los Escombros\,” which was part of the Grammy-winning Best Musica Urbana Album 2024. Over the years\, he has collaborated with a cross-section of leading artists\, including Simon Shaheen\, Amir ElSaffar\, Roger Waters\, Residente\, G.E. Smith\, and Fabrizio Cassol.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261004T000000Z
SUMMARY:Firas Zreik’s Tarabesque: New Journeys in Arabic Music (RBA)
UID:0681c9fd-7bb1-4580-9b77-a96cd11f051b@0681.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/firas-zreik-arabic-music-rba/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $42.23\nPurchase Tickets\nTerre Thaemlitz presents a debut live performance on piano\, followed by a conversation with Ciarán Finlayson\, as part of DURATIONS Festival.\n\nThe legendary house producer\, DJ\, and writer\nTerre Thaemlitz [aka DJ Sprinkles]\nwill perform her first composition for solo piano live at Roulette. The performance will be followed by a conversation with Triple Canopy contributing editor\nCiarán Finlayson\n\, who will speak with Thaemlitz about her music as well as her criticism\, which often takes up the defects of a culture industry that offers artists “exposure” instead of wages.\nPresented in collaboration with Triple Canopy.\nRoulette members – please email\nmembership@roulette.org\nfor more information about member discounts.\nTriple Canopy\nis committed to cultivating unconventional\, unassimilated forms of thought and expression\, and to meaningfully supporting the people who produce them. The magazine collaborates with contributors on artworks\, essays\, fictions\, conversations\, performances\, and books [among other media] from conception to realization\, navigating the digital and physical realms where ideas and audiences take shape. In doing so\, Triple Canopy strives to not only analyze but alter the structures that influence whose voices are heard\, whose stories are circulated\, and whose experiences are valued.\nFounded in 2007 and based in New York City\, Triple Canopy is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit organization\, has been certified by W.A.G.E.\, and is a member of the Coalition of Small Arts New York City (CoSA NYC).\nDURATIONS Festival\nis a week-long\, multi-disciplinary exploration of sound and connection. Now in its fifth iteration\, the festival’s programming is its most robust yet\, with seven days of music\, talks\, and audiovisual work taking place between Public Records\, Pioneer Works\, Roulette\, and Whitney Museum of American Art.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261116T010000Z
SUMMARY:DURATIONS: An Evening with Terre Thaemlitz [Live Piano] + Conversation with Ciarán Finlayson
UID:42651fd2-ebb4-40e1-a884-6f6c924211db@4265.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/durations-triple-canopy-terre-thaemlitz-ciaran-finlayson/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nTwo evenings bring together artists representing the radical spirit that continues to shape the landscape of contemporary music.\n\nA two-night mini-festival celebrating the 80th anniversary of the Internationales Musikinstitut Darmstadt’s groundbreaking Summer Course for New Music—the global meeting point for postwar experimentation\, exchange\, and creative risk.\nNight two features the New York premiere of legendary composer and NEA Jazz Master\nAnthony Braxton’s\nmost recent concert work\,\nThunder Music\n\, which debuted at Darmstadt in 2023\, performed by a group including members of\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble\n.\nGBSR Duo\nopen the evening with a performance of\nKarlheinz Stockhausen’s\nKontakte\n(1958-60).\nThe festival will also include archival listening sessions and lectures exploring Darmstadt’s historic legacy and its living\, evolving influence on today’s artistic practice. Additional details to be announced.\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble x Tri-Centric plays Anthony Braxton’s Thunder Music\nCarl Testa\ndouble bass and electronics\nLevy Lorenzo\npercussion\nIngrid Laubrock\nsaxophone\nDarius Jones\nsaxophone\nReut Regev\ntrombone\nChris McIntyre\ntrombone\nRebekah Heller\nbassoon and conductor\nJonathan Finlayson\ntrumpet\nNick Hallett\nvoice\nKyoko Kitamura\nvoice\nRaquel Klein\nvoice\nAdam Matlock\nvoice and accordion\nAnne Rhodes\nvoice\nFay Victor\nvoice\nGBSR Duo plays Stockhausen’s Kontakte\nGeorge Barton\npercussion\nSiwan Rhys\npiano\n“While not strictly dramatic in nature\,\nThunder Music\nsuggested a stagelike feel. In this new category of (Braxton’s) compositional practice\, individual musicians are responsible for making choices about how to merge their own sound with prerecorded sounds of thunder and nature.”—\nNew York Times in 2023\nOrganized by\nNick Hallett\nand\nZach Layton\n\, curators of NYC’s Darmstadt: Classics of the Avant-Garde\, together with\nThomas Schäfer\n\, director of the IMD\, and\nGeorge Lewis\n\, Artistic Director of the International Contemporary Ensemble\, the festival bridges generations of experimental sound. Envisioned in partnership with a consortium including\nRoulette Intermedium\, The New School\, Fridman Gallery\,\nand the\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nInternational Contemporary Ensemble\nis a leading contemporary music based in New York\, currently directed by George Lewis. Founded in 2001 and led by instrumentalists from Ohio’s Oberlin Conservatory including Claire Chase\, Joshua Rubin\, Ross Karre\, and Rebekah Heller\, the ensemble features a flexible collective of instrumentalists and vocalists performing repertoire ranging from solo works to large ensemble pieces. Praised by\nThe New York Times\nas “one of the most accomplished and adventurous groups in new music\,” the Ensemble has premiered more than 500 compositions\, many by emerging composers\, in venues worldwide. The ensemble is recognized for innovative programming\, interdisciplinary collaboration\, and educational initiatives that connect new music with diverse audiences. The Ensemble has received numerous honors\, including the American Music Center Trailblazer Award and Musical America’s 2013 Ensemble of the Year award. The Ensemble has collaborated with conductors including John Adams and Susanna Mälkki and performs internationally at major festivals and concert halls.\nGBSR Duo\nPercussionist George Barton and pianist Siwan Rhys are one of the UK’s leading contemporary chamber ensembles\, celebrated for fearless performances\, ambitious commissions\, and inventive collaborations. Their repertoire spans twentieth-century modernism\, experimental music\, and cross-genre projects\, with programs featuring composers from Karlheinz Stockhausen and Galina Ustvolskaya to Brian Eno and Aphex Twin. The duo has premiered works by Eva-Maria Houben\, Cassie Kinoshi\, Linda Catlin Smith\, and Oliver Leith. They are winners of the Royal Philharmonic Society 2025 Young Artist Award. GBSR has performed internationally with ensembles and artists including the Los Angeles Philharmonic\, Basel Sinfonietta\, and Thomas Adès. In 2026\, they serve as Artists in Residence at Kings Place.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261212T010000Z
SUMMARY:Darmstadt at 80 Festival: International Contemporary Ensemble x Tri-Centric plays Anthony Braxton’s Thunder Music // GBSR Duo plays Stockhausen’s Kontakte
UID:420668e9-5dc6-42f3-a2d6-72b325262ba3@4206.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/darmstadt-at-80-international-contemporary-ensemble-x-tri-centric-anthony-braxtons-thunder-music/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:S\nō\nPercussion\ncelebrates the life of Tim Thomas (1966–2026)\, who passed away in January of this year. This evening honors his love of music and of the community he was such a positive force within through Sō Laboratories—the series he loved dearly and guided with passion.\nS\nō\nPercussion + Friends\nMatt Evans\npercussion\nAmy Garapic\npercussion\nCharlotte Greve\nalto saxophone\nSruly Lazaros\nDani Matei\npercussion\nBeth Meyers\nviola\nDrew Neal\npercussion\nDylan Offrais\npercussion\nPat Swoboda\nbass\nPhilippa Thompson\n“Through a mix of consummate skill and quirky charm\, this mercurial quartet has helped to ignite an explosive new enthusiasm for percussion music old and new.” – The New York Times\n“…This is music-making at its most vital\, expressive and imaginative.” – BBC Music Magazine\n“A marvelous program…crisply sensitive performances…This is a group that clearly inspires composers to push the limits of what’s possible.” – San Francisco Chronicle\nPresented by Sō Percussion in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nFor 25 years and counting\, Grammy-winning percussion quartet\nSō Percussion\nhas redefined chamber music for the 21st century through an “exhilarating blend of precision and anarchy\, rigor and bedlam” (\nThe New Yorker\n). They are celebrated by audiences and presenters for a dazzling range of work: for live performances in which “telepathic powers of communication” (\nThe New York Times\n) bring to life the vibrant percussion repertoire\; for an extravagant array of collaborations in classical music\, pop\, indie rock\, contemporary dance\, and theater\; and for their work in education and community-building\, seeking to explore the immense possibility of art in our time.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260916T000000Z
SUMMARY:Sō Laboratories in Memory of Tim Thomas
UID:768dfa5d-2e54-409d-bbfc-3e56119eb5bf@768d.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/so-laboratories-in-memory-of-tim-thomas/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $20\nPurchase Tickets\nThe Interpretations series returns with sets by acclaimed pianist Ursel Schlicht and Jason Kao Hwang's jazz trio.\n\nThe Interpretations Series continues its 37th season at Roulette with a solo performance by pianist\nUrsel Schlicht\nand a set from the jazz trio Critical Response\, featuring\nJason Kao Hwang\non violin and viola\,\nAnders Nilsson\non guitar\, and\nMichael T.A. Thompson\non drums and percussion.\nPresented by Interpretations in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nThe Interpretations series\nis a New York-based concert series focusing on the relationship between contemporary composers and their interpreters. Sometimes the interpreters are the composers themselves\; more often\, the series features performers who specialize in the interpretation of new music. Since its inception in 1989\, Interpretations has featured leading figures in contemporary music and multimedia\, including Muhal Richard Abrams\, Robert Ashley\, Anthony Braxton\, Thomas Buckner\, FLUX Quartet\, Joseph Kubera\, Annea Lockwood\, and Alvin Lucier\, Roscoe Mitchell\, Phill Niblock\, Pauline Oliveros\, Ursula Oppens\, and Morton Subotnick.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261023T000000Z
SUMMARY:Interpretations: Ursel Schlicht // Critical Response
UID:ba40d21f-99fe-4350-a508-3fc14405d5e0@ba40.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/interpretations-ursel-schlicht-critical-response/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nThree performers collide\, spin\, fall\, and intertwine in an athletic maze of child’s play and menacing acts.\n\nHow Dogs Play\nexplores the complex presence of aggression within intimacy — a crude slip between the human and the monstrous\, child’s play and menacing acts. Three performers\, Amelia Heintzelman\, Brendan Elefante\, and Jack Dexter collide\, spin\, fall\, and intertwine in an athletic maze. Sounds are amplified and live processed\, exaggerating the tussles of everyday life\, tendered in a soundscape by Tommy Martinez.\nAmelia Heintzelman\nchoreographer and performer\nBrendan Elefante\nperformer\nJack Dexter\nperformer\nTommy Martinez\nsound artist\nTess Michaelson\ndramaturg\n“The sheer viscerality of her presence\, which seems to have mounted from her very depths\, is commanding.” – Tess Michaelson\, Brooklyn Rail\n“Her reverence for form and spectacle is felt in the deeply moving strokes of her physicality. Instead of making meaning\, she finds it in the corners that the edges of her movement create.” – Sophia Parker\, Culturebot\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nAmelia Heintzelman\nis a dancer\, choreographer\, and teacher. Her work has been supported by residencies\, commissions\, and grants through Center for Performance Research\, Draftwork at Danspace Project\, Foundation for Contemporary Arts\, Issue Project Room\, Movement Research at the Judson Church\, New York Foundation for the Arts\, Pageant\, Snug Harbor\, and UCross Foundation. Most recently\, she’s worked as a collaborating performer for Kim Brandt\, Jesi Cook\, Ayano Elson\, Deborah Hay\, Melinda Ring and Alexa West. She is on teaching faculty at Movement Research and Pageant\, and rarely/sometimes teaches Comedy Pilates.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261203T010000Z
SUMMARY:Amelia Heintzelman: How Dogs Play (Night 2)
UID:b5693f27-9454-4e3d-bda9-eb86fc0bb3ab@b569.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/amelia-heintzelman-how-dogs-play-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nA celebration of the groundbreaking work of video artist Kit Fitzgerald\, showcasing her lush visual style in two U.S. premieres.\n\nRoulette presents the work of video artist\nKit Fitzgerald\n\, showcasing her lush visual style in two US premieres:\nVenezia: Dream of Consciousness\n\, a new-narrative video made with composer\nPeter Gordon\n\, featuring the noted actor\nTomas Arana\non a nocturnal search through the back streets of Venice\; and\nOutside the Box (Yellow)\n\, a performance that explores the parameters of beauty and abstraction through a real-time mix of video drawing\, image processing\, and sensuality. Featured musicians in\nOutside the Box (Yellow)\ninclude Peter Gordon\, Max Gordon\, and Blake Leyh.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nKit Fitzgerald\nhas created groundbreaking works of video art and live video performance. She collaborated with musicians\, choreographers\, and theater and opera directors\, and also directed music videos and documentaries. Fitzgerald’s work is in the collection of The Museum of Modern Art and was twice included in the Whitney Biennial. She received grants and commissions from The Rockefeller Foundation\, the National Endowment for the Arts\, Tokyo Broadcasting System\, NHK\, and Sony Japan. Festival prizes included firsts at the International Electronic Cinema Festival (Montreux)\, Tokyo International HDTV Festival\, and International Women’s Biennale (Vienna). Her work is distributed by Electronic Arts Intermix\, New York.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261206T010000Z
SUMMARY:Kit Fitzgerald
UID:6e0b9d8b-a997-4adc-8d24-5c64297628fb@6e0b.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/kit-fitzgerald/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nBassoonist shares an intuitive convergence of sound\, spirit\, and radical self-expression.\n\nJoy Guidry’s\nFive Prayer\ns is an intuitive convergence of sound\, spirit\, and radical self-expression. A celebrated voice in experimental and avant-garde music spaces\, Guidry pushes sonic boundaries rooted in Black sonic traditions. Her newest album\,\nFive Prayers\,\nshifts away from free jazz and into a meditative\, ambient realm. Guidry’s live performance blends bassoon with low rumbling synths\, warm electronic sounds\, praise breaks\, distortion\, and heavy bass that reflect the artist’s lived experience as a Black trans woman from the South.\nFive Prayers\nis a profound act of self-exploration and liberation\, offering audiences a raw\, vulnerable\, and transcendent musical journey.\nJoy Guidry\nbassoon\, electronics\nScott Li\nviolin\, synth\n+Additional performers TBA\n“With productions that sound as if they belong to a film soundtrack\, Texas’ Joy Guidry is crafting elegant and joyous music that sits somewhere at the intersection of ambient\, jazz\, and experimental.” – Mixmag\n“Joy Guidry’s music is an evocation of prayer … [that] sits at the nexus between Black classical music\, modern European traditions\, alt soul\, jazz\, and beyond.” – Clash Magazine\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nJoy Guidry\nis a bassoonist\, versatile improviser\, performance artist\, and composer of experimental\, daring new works that embody a deep love of storytelling\; Joy’s music channels her inner child in honor of her ancestors and predecessors. Joy was born in Houston\, Texas\, into a creative family that has shaped who she is today. Joy is the founder of Jaid Records\, which is a record label that focuses on experimental ambient music and free jazz. Jaid Records was founded in October of 2024\, and currently has released her third album titled\nFive Prayers\n.\nJoy has presented her original work at The Metropolitan Museum of Art\, Park Avenue Armory\, Kraftwerk\, Whitney Museum for American Art\, Hauser & Wirth\, Museum of Contemporary Art San Diego\, Cafe Oto\, The Kitchen\, Redcat\, among many other venues. She has been commissioned by The National Sawdust\, JACK Quartet\, Gaudeamus Festival\, and the I&I Foundation. Joy has been featured in festivals like Berlin Atonal\, La Biennale di Venezia\, London jazz Festival\, Banlieues Bleues festival\, A’Larme Festival\, Cologne Jazz Week\, Dripping Festival\, Big Ears Festival\, and many more. Joy Guidry is the winner of the 2021 Berlin Prize for Young Artists. She is currently playing on a Heckel Bassoon number 6101.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260917T000000Z
SUMMARY:Joy Guidry: Five Prayers in Five Movements
UID:1802e7b6-7a4f-43ca-9839-ce5582c6ca7a@1802.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/joy-guidry-five-prayers-in-five-movements/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nA duet of video game visuals\, modular synth\, guitar\, and voice exploring the psycho-sexual inner world of a girl-child in the '80s.\n\nArtist/musician\nErika M. Anderson (EMA)\nand visual artist\nTabitha Nikolai\npremiere\nMemory As A Rock Out Of Reach\,\nan hour long duet performance that uses video game technologies to build out the psycho-sexual inner world of a girl-child in the ’80s. Soundtracked by modular synth and guitar\, a narrator searches for her shape amid commercial detritus\, degraded 3D scans of suburbia\, and a Polly Pocket apocalypse.\nErika Anderson\nmusic\, sound\, narration\nTabitha Nikolai\nlive visuals\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nErika M. Anderson (EMA)\nand\nTabitha Nikolai\ncreate live digital experiences that transcend genre. With a mixed background of DIY music\, art\, and hacked together immersive technology\, the duo investigate the potential for visceral\, emotive storytelling using tools often reserved for slick AAA sedation. Their individual work has been featured in places like MoMA PS1\, the Barbican Centre\, and the Journal of Gaming and Virtual Worlds.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261031T000000Z
SUMMARY:Memory as a Rock Out of Reach: EMA and Tabitha Nikolai
UID:b395707a-85e4-473c-869b-5230e3feb86b@b395.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/erika-m-anderson-ema-tabitha-nikolai/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nTwo ensembles create collective sound-worlds deeply rooted in the spirit of improvisation.\n\nAn evening featuring sets by\nNate Wooley’s Mutual Aid Music\nand\nLarry Ochs Safe Havens (for the Abstract Truth)\n\, each consisting of musicians committed to collective music-making\, where listening and contributing to a spontaneous but reflective order\nmatters\n.\nBoth groups come together around the ultimate goal of improvised music\, which is to be as strong as its musicians’ collective concentration\, listening\, and immediate\, magical responses. The spirit of improvisation has inspired and intrigued Ochs since the beginning and is echoed in Nate Wooley’s writings about about Mutual Aid Music: “It is a music in which each person is asked to perform within the mission of musical mutual aid: giving their sound to the group with the goal of building a musical statement – and language – that transcends the individual\, strong personalities that make up the ensemble.”\nMutual Aid Music\nNate Wooley\ntrumpet\nModney\nviolin\nLester St. Louis\ncello\nMatt Moran\nvibraphone\nLuke Stewart\nbass\nRussell Greenberg\npercussion\nSafe Havens (for the Abstract Truth)\nLarry Ochs\ntenor\, sopranino saxophones\nCharles Burnham\nviolin\nTrevor Dunn\nacoustic\, electric bass and effects\nIkue Mori\nelectronics\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nNate Wooley\nis an improvisor\, composer\, and writer. As an instrumentalist\, he has been a soloist with the New York Philharmonic as well as working with composers such as Annea Lockwood\, Éliane Radigue\, and Anthony Braxton. His solo trumpet improvisations have been hailed as “exquisitely hostile” and “the most progressive campfire music ever.” His compositions are evolving amalgams of improvisation\, folk music\, text\, and immersive experiences that communicate an arc of human experience from the fragility of being alone to the ecstasy of collectivity.\nMutual Aid Music\nis a compositional ecosystem of small works for a flexible chamber ensemble of improvisers. Based on over 100 micro-compositions\, the music is assembled in the moment according to the rules of the piece. Each player randomly chooses a page of music\, which they then use as the primary material from which to improvise a part of the overall. One performer uses no material at all\, and must improvise their part from scratch. The goal is to react to\, reinforce\, and highlight the contributions of the rest of the group using only the limited musical prompts given on the chosen micro-composition. The result is a chamber music that is amoebic\, ever-changing and shifting in three-dimensions as each musician manipulates their material to best fit the group’s sound in the present.\nMutual Aid Music\nis a prime example of my belief that profound musical complexity comes from the social act of performing music together\; each performance—whether it is from a trio or a full chamber orchestra—is made solely from each musician’s education\, aesthetic\, and virtuosity interacting in real time with the same traits of their partners. In this communal way of making music\, the most wonderful and surprising sounds can be produced\nLarry Ochs\nmakes music in the worlds of “avant-garde jazz” aka “improvised music\,” composing music for bands like Rova Saxophone 4tet (with whom he has performed since 1978)\,  Larry Ochs Sax & Drumming Core\, Ochs-Robinson Duo\, The Fictive Five\, and Kihnoua. All-improv bands have seen him partner and tour with Nels Cline\, Gerald Cleaver\, Mark Dresser\, Vladimir Tarasov\, Dave Rempis\, Lisa Mezzacappa\, Darren Johnston\, Ben Davis\, Miya Masaoka\, Fred Frith\, Joe Morris\, Chris Brown\, William Winant\, Joan Jeanrenaud\, and Peggy Lee\, among others.\nSafe Havens (for the Abstract Truth)\nLarry Ochs dreamed up this quartet for a show at The Stone in February 2025 with gabby fluke-mogul on violin. That same quartet recorded at Roulette in July 2025. While listening to the final mixes of recordings in Roulette’s basement studio\, Ochs fell into reverie of the musicians’ consistent focus and the vibe of the music. It was during that time that he came up with the name of the band which references the title of the great jazz record\nBlues and the Abstract Truth\n. While the music the group plays is not at all like the music on that important recording\, it is imbued with a vibe like a blues\, and it has its own abstract truth about it. It’s own integrity.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261001T000000Z
SUMMARY:Nate Wooley’s Mutual Aid Music // Larry Ochs Safe Havens (for the Abstract Truth)
UID:48d9a6a4-8c37-48f0-830a-1dfc23986a3e@48d9.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/nate-wooley-larry-ochs/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Triple Canopy’s annual symposium is a manifestation of the magazine’s concerns and collaborations\, in person and in real time. The multiday gathering brings together artists\, writers\, performers\, and researchers to address the role of culture in responding (or contributing) to a range of critical issues. The full lineup will be announced soon. RSVP to stay updated.\nThe 2026 Triple Canopy Symposium is supported by Critical Minded and will be hosted in multiple venues with additional dates presented in partnership with Public Records’s Durations festival.\nmore info\nHERE.\nTriple Canopy\nis a New York-based magazine that works with artists and writers to address the critical issues that define contemporary life.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261115T010000Z
SUMMARY:Triple Canopy Symposium 2026 (Day 2)
UID:eedb0c65-05f3-4a59-bf17-da7a22a2f7fb@eedb.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/triple-canopy-symposium-2026-day-2/
END:VEVENT
