BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nTwo jazz legends present a rare duo concert\, putting to fore the consummate magic of their deeply developed communion.\n\nMatthew Shipp and William Parker present a rare duo concert celebrating their boundless four-decade collaboration. Their new studio album\,\nA Whole System Of\n—their fourth duo recording—will be released on November 6 via TAO Forms. On the same day\, their first duo album\,\nZo\n\, will be reissued by ESP-Disk’\, full circle style.\nThe intimacy of the duo will wholly feature the consummate magic of their deeply developed communion.\nMatthew Shipp\npiano\nWilliam Parker\nbass\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nMatthew Shipp\nis recognized for having forged a unique pianistic language within an expansive body of work that bridges the avant-garde tradition with 21st-century sonic landscapes\, expanding the expressive possibilities of jazz in the process.\nA renowned master of the double bass\,\nWilliam Parker\nis also recognized for his lifelong dedication to free jazz as a spiritual practice and community-building force — work that has ensured the continuity of an essential art form and fostered a supportive ecosystem for generations of creative musicians.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261205T010000Z
SUMMARY:Matthew Shipp & William Parker: A Whole System Of
UID:5af49fc9-6dc2-4e05-9b64-d2eb0339230f@5af4.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/matthew-shipp-william-parker-duo-a-whole-system-of/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nThe 16th consecutive year of Niblock’s meditative installation—numerous video projections soundtracked by his iconic and permeating drone.\n\n6pm-midnight\n*Tickets allow re-entry.*\nAs the longest night of the year unfolds\, Roulette stages Phill Niblock’s annual Winter Solstice concert for the 16th consecutive year\, in collaboration with his partner\, video artist Katherine Liberovskaya.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nPhill Niblock\n(1933-2024\, USA) was an artist whose fifty-year career spanned minimalist and experimental music\, film\, and photography. Since 1985\, he served as director of Experimental Intermedia\, a foundation for avant-garde music based in New York with a branch in Ghent\, and curator of the foundation’s record label XI. Known for his thick\, loud drones of music\, Niblock’s signature sound is filled with microtones of instrumental timbres that generate many other tones in the performance space. In 2013\, his diverse artistic career was the subject of a retrospective realized in partnership between Circuit (Contemporary Art Centre Lausanne) and Musée de l’Elysée. The following year Niblock was honored with the prestigious Foundation for Contemporary Arts John Cage Award.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261221T230000Z
SUMMARY:Phill Niblock Winter Solstice 2026: 6 Hours of Music and Film
UID:e3b68265-a0d8-481f-b699-8ca24e92d9ab@e3b6.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/phill-niblock-winter-solstice-2026/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nBaird and Williams celebrate their first duo album on Outside Time Records\, opened by an improvised cello and bass duo.\n\nKa Baird\nand\nChris Ryan Williams\ncelebrate the release of\nEnthalpy\n\, their first duo album on Outside Time Records (out Sept. 25\, 2026).\nReflecting on the album\, Jonathan Williger writes:\n“The music flies out in bursts\; it seeps and oozes\, flows with intention. It is an incredibly rhythmic work without any hint of meter\, full of sounds that punctuate with the thud of a dart hitting a bullseye. Williams’ capability to conjure swirling atmospherics\, as well as his remarkable ability to pull fragments of melody from the haze\, fit puzzle-like into Baird’s exclamations and the eruption of electronics that often dovetail them.”\nKa Baird\nflutes\, voice\, electronics\nChris Ryan Williams\ntrumpet\, electronics\n“Ka Baird’s music occupies the space where\, by\nbreathing\, the everyday meets the sublime.\n” –\nResident Advisor\n“What kind of thing—what type of music\, what\ncategory of experience—is Chris Williams’ Odu:\nVibration II?…. Descending into its hidden depths\, you\nmight decide that it’s an exercise in deep listening or\nan explosion of linear timekeeping.\n”\n– Pitchfork\nCellist/composer\nLeila Bordreuil\nand bassist/composer\nLuke Stewart\nplay a duo set exploring feedback\, improvisation\, and the expanded sonic possibilities of their instruments.\nLeila Bordreuil\ncello\nLuke Stewart\ndouble bass\n“Bordreuil creates steadily scathing music\, favoring long and corrosive atonalities.”—New York Times\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nChris Ryan Williams\nis an artist and musician based in Brooklyn\, NY whose work unfolds through electroacoustic composition and performance installation. His EP\nLiv\ne earned praise from Jazz Right Now and The Quietus for its “dazzling collaged pieces that ricochet between improvised passages and written material” (Peter Margasak\, The Quietus). His debut LP\nOdu: Vibration II\n\, praised by Pitchfork and named one of NPR’s Best New Albums\, reimagines a journey through Plato’s cave.\nKa Baird\nis a performer\, sound artist\, musician and composer based in New York City.  Using extended voice and microphone techniques along with electronics\, feedback\, field recordings\, and flutes\, they explore the outer dimensions of sound through performance. They create a present tense sound with a vigorous\, ritualistic delivery that seeks extreme release through physical exertion and psychic extension. They have collaborated with many other artists\, both in structured compositions and through their dedicated practice of improvisation. Their solo  releases include\nSapropelic Pycnic\n(Drag City 2017)\,\nRespires\n(RVNG Intl 2019)\,\nBrooding Exercises\n(Longform Editions 2021)\,\nVivification Exercises\n(RVNG Intl 2021)\, and\nBearings: Soundtracks for the Bardos\n(RVNG 2024). They have been a recipient of the Foundation of Contemporary Art’s Emergency Grant\,  a Jerome Foundation Artist-In-Residence at Roulette Intermedium\, and was a Jerome Hill Artist Fellow through 2023-25.\nLeila Bordreuil’s\nmusic accesses concepts from noise\, free jazz\, contemporary classical and other experimental traditions but adheres to no single genre. Mixing melancholic cello melodies with harsh noise walls\, she creates “steadily scathing music\, favoring long and corrosive atonalities.” (New York Times). Driven by a fierce interest in pure sound and inherent texture\, Leila challenges conventional cello practice through extreme extended techniques and amplification methods\, to the extent she sometimes seems to be playing the P.A rather than her cello. Her composed works frequently incorporate sound-spatialization by way of site-specific pieces and multichannel installations. Her panoramic vision of form and genre is reflected in the diversity of her collaborative projects\, which include artists such as Tamio Shiraishi (Fushitsuta)\, Lee Ranaldo (Sonic Youth)\, Kali Malone\, Laurel Halo\, Drew McDowall (Coil)\, Zach Rowden (Tongue Depressor)\, Micheal Foster\, Julia Santoli\, and Luke Stewart (Irreversible Entanglements).\nLuke Stewart\nis a musician\, performer\, improviser-composer\, organizer\, and writer-researcher whose work represents a deep reverence for the history and tradition of Creative Music: a tradition which encompasses the diverse styles of expression within the body of Black Music in the United States\, Africa\, and throughout the world. Stewart’s regular ensembles include Irreversible Entanglements\, SILT Trio\, Exposure Quintet\, and the experimental rock duo Blacks’ Myths\; he also performs regularly in numerous collaborations. Over the years\, Stewart has performed at Arts for Art’s Vision Festival\, New York\, NY\; Winter Jazzfest\, New York\, NY\; The Kennedy Center\, Washington\, D.C\; Rhizome DC\, Washington\, D.C.\; The Metropolitan Museum of Art\, New York\, NY\; Big Ears Festival\, Knoxville\, TN\; the BIMHUIS\, Amsterdam\, Netherlands\; and Roskilde Festival\, Roskilde\, Denmark\, among many other venues and festivals in the United States and abroad.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261218T010000Z
SUMMARY:Ka Baird & Chris Ryan Williams “Enthalpy” Record Release // Leila Bordreuil & Luke Stewart
UID:333e7650-8128-466f-a325-b7d4760d565b@333e.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/ka-baird-chris-ryan-williams-leila-bordreuil-luke-stewart/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:To mark the occasion of Ronen Givony’s new book\,\nUs v. Them: The Age of Indie Music and a Decade in New York (2004-2014)\n\,\nRoulette presents a two-night event highlighting the Brooklyn music scene and DIY community of the early aughts.\nNight one celebrates the DIY venue Silent Barn with performances by\nDavid Longstreth (Dirty Projectors)\n\,\nHaley Fohr\n(Circuit des Yeux)\n\,\nMal Devisa\n\, and the\nWordless Music String Quartet\,\npreceded by a short discussion and reading from\nUs v. Them\n(panelists to be announced).\nPresented by Wordless Music and BrooklynVegan.\nChoose the ticket level that feels right for you\n. All proceeds from the evening support Roulette’s programs and mission to present and preserve extraordinary experimental music\, movement\, and media—and above all\, support artists.\n$35\n— General Admission\n$65\n— General Admission + 2 drink tickets\n$95\n— General Admission + 2 drink tickets\nThis is a special benefit to support Roulette. Member and Patron benefits do not apply—thank you for your support!\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nDavid Longstreth\nSince 2002\, when David Longstreth began self-releasing critically-acclaimed albums from his college dorm\, DIRTY PROJECTORS has grown to be one of the most innovative bands in indie rock\, pushing sonic boundaries and blazing unexpected paths. Dirty Projectors’ list of collaborators include Bjork and David Byrne\, and Longstreth has co-written/produced with Solange\, Bombino\, and Rihanna/Kanye West/Paul McCartney on the worldwide hit “FourFiveSeconds.” The band has played at most of the major music festivals in the world\, and countless venues\, big and small. The most recent album\,\nSong of the Earth\n(2025) was recorded by Dirty Projectors with the chamber orchestra s t a r g a z e. The Los Angeles Philharmonic performed the piece’s US premiere in a sold out show at Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2024.  Longstreth scored two Hollywood films in 2025 — the orchestral score for\nThe Legend of Ochi\n\, and a piano score for\nLove Me\n. Longstreth is currently at work on two Dirty Projectors albums and scores for percussion quartet and chamber orchestra.\nHaley Fohr\nis a vocalist\, composer and singer-songwriter based in Chicago\, Illinois. Her musical endeavors focus around our human condition\, and her fifteen-year career as Circuit des Yeux has grown into one of America’s most successful efforts to connect the personal to the universal. She is most distinctly identified by her 4-octave voice and unique style of 12-string guitar. Her recent works include an original soundtrack for Charles Bryant’s silent film\nSalomé\n(1923)\, commissioned by Opera North\, her critically acclaimed 2021 album\n-io\,\nand two LPs under the mysterious moniker of Jackie Lynn.\nMal Devisa\nis the songwriting\, liberation\, and poetry project of multifarious artist Deja Carr. Starting in 2014 and breaking through with 2016’s\nKiid\n\, Mal Devisa’s work spans a self-made spectrum of sound from gravitic\, soulful rock to soliloquy to unabashed hip hop. Although known for her unmistakable\, smoldering voice and loop-based\, bass-forward compositions\, Carr’s talents also extend to reaches of spoken word and production\, paralleled by aspirations to start both a youth foundation and Afrobeat orchestra. Such boundless inspiration is a central facet of Mal Devisa’s work\, whose sonically and narratively unrestrained passages teem with empathy and liberatory visions for a better world.\nWordless Music Orchestra\nis the house band of New York’s Wordless Music series\, which was founded by non-musician Ronen Givony in 2006\, and has since presented concerts around the world\, pairing artists from the sound worlds of classical and contemporary music. Comprising New York’s most omnivorous young musicians\, the orchestra has performed at the Sydney Opera House\, Madison Square Garden\, Brooklyn Academy of Music\, and London’s Barbican Center\, and collaborated with artists ranging from Sigur Rós\, Spiritualized\, Jonny Greenwood\, Mica Levi\, Ludwig Göransson\, Jason Moran\, Joanna Newsom\, Dev Hynes\, Nicholas Britell\, and Jóhann Jóhannsson to Paul Thomas Anderson\, Barry Jenkins\, Terrence Malick\, and Jane Campion.\nUs v. Them\nThe Age of Indie Music and a Decade in New York (2004-2014)\nIn the tradition of\nJust Kids\nand\nOur Band Could Be Your Life\n\, Ronen Givony’s\nUs v. Them\nchronicles the generation of young artists who came to Brooklyn in the mid-2000s: a small but seismic scene that coalesced under a billionaire mayor\, a series of forever wars\, and a music industry in free fall. Through reporting\, research\, and interviews with musicians\, industry insiders\, and individuals from\nPitchfork\n\, Vice\, Scion\, and the Red Bull Music Academy\,\nUs v. Them\nexamines the rise and fall of indie music in a post-Napster landscape\, marked by vast disruption in technology\, politics\, economics\, journalism\, and patronage.\nSupporting Roulette = Supporting Artists\nRoulette was founded by artists for artists\, and has stayed true to that ethos for nearly five decades. We strive to be the best place in New York City for artists at every stage in their careers\, presenting over 100 concerts a year\, paying artists guaranteed fees\, and preserving their work in our extensive public archive. Through our Awarded Artist program\, we have distributed over $2.3M in commissioning\, residency\, and fellowship fees to artists developing new work\, many of whom have gone on to become leaders in their fields or later recognized as MacArthur Fellows\, Pulitzer Prize winners\, and NEA Jazz masters. A ticket for this benefit concert goes directly to supporting our mission of supporting artists making new\, experimental work.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261002T000000Z
SUMMARY:Us v. Them: Celebrating Silent Barn w/ David Longstreth\, Haley Fohr\, Mal Devisa\, and the Wordless Music String Quartet (Roulette Benefit)
UID:d8557cb4-bbe9-46af-8475-6918022f628f@d855.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/us-v-them-silent-barn-dave-longstreth-haley-fohr-wordless-music-string-quartet/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $30\nPurchase Tickets\nAn improvised structure for an orchestra of voices\, electric guitars\, and an audience\, using the Bill of Rights as its source material.\n\nIn advance of the midterm elections composer/improviser Carla Kihlstedt and poet Rafael Osés present\nX Amendments\n\,\nan improvised sonic structure for an orchestra of voices\, electric guitars\, and an audience\, u\nsing the first ten amendments of the United States Constitution – The Bill of Rights – as its source material. The performance is an experiment in individual and collective freedoms and an exploration of rights\, responsibilities\, agency\, and restrictions.\nHolland Andrews\nvoice\nGelsey Bell\nvoice\nCarla Kihlstedt\nvoice\nAnaïs Maviel\nvoice\nRafael Osés\nvoice\nDither:\nTaylor Levine\nguitar\nJoshua Lopes\nguitar\nJames Moore\nguitar\nBrendon Randall-Myers\nguitar\nBrandon Ross\nguitar\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nCarla Kihlstedt\nis an American composer\, improviser\, writer and educator who plays violin/viola and sings\, often simultaneously. She loves the economy\, intimacy\, and unapologetic directness of song. In her larger pieces (\nBlack Inscription\, Necessary Monsters\, 26 Little Deaths\, At Night We Walk in Circles and Are Consumed by Fire\,\nPandæmonium\, X Amendments\, 6 Dioramas) she explores the complexity of a single idea through many different lenses.\nCarla is a founding member of the experimental metal band Sleepytime Gorilla Museum\, the composers collective Tin Hat (formerly Tin Hat Trio)\, the song-based projects Rabbit Rabbit Radio and 2 Foot Yard\, and the improvised bands Causing A Tiger\, and Minamo. She is on the faculty of the Contemporary Musical Arts Department of the New England Conservatory\, the MFA in Music Composition program at the Vermont College of Fine Arts at CalArts\, and she co-facilitated the Creative Gesture Lab for composers and choreographers at the Banff Centre for Arts and Creativity. She is currently incubating two new choral projects: Long for This World—an educational\, environmentally-centered youth chorus project\; Feral Choir—an experimental vocal group rooted in the physicality of singing bodies.\nDither\n\, a New York based electric guitar quartet\, is dedicated to an eclectic mix of experimental repertoire which spans composed\, improvised\, and electronic music. Formed in 2007\, the quartet has performed across the United States and abroad\, presenting new commissions\, original compositions\, multimedia works\, and large guitar ensemble pieces. Dither’s members are Taylor Levine\, Joshua Lopes\, James Moore\, and Brendon Randall-Myers.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261008T000000Z
SUMMARY:X AMENDMENTS: Carla Kihlstedt\, Rafael Osés\, and Dither (Night 1)
UID:d23a16b7-bc73-4922-a900-8cb74fea5a4c@d23a.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/x-amendments-carla-kihlstedt-rafael-oses-dither/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nInteractive sound art pioneer + painter/sculptor celebrate Harvestworks’ 50th\, plus a convo with e-flux Performance Curator Sanna Almajedi.\n\nHarvestworks and Roulette are pleased to co-present\nLive Processing Paint and Sound\n\, a performance by pioneer of interactive sound art\nLiz Phillips\nand painter\nHeidi Howard\n. The program\, presented as part of Harvestworks’ 50th anniversary celebration\, will include an introduction by Harvestworks’ Interim Director\nIvana Dama\n\, followed by a conversation with\nSanna Almajedi\n\, Performance Curator at e-flux.\nThroughout the creation of Heidi Howard’s painting\, Liz Phillips will live-process aquatic recordings using Mugic and light sensors\, contact microphones\, and a theremin\, transforming brushstrokes and fish activity into an immersive water soundscape. Live video synthesis from six camera and screen feeds will illuminate Roulette’s large projection screen.\nLiz Phillips\nsynthesizers and custom electronics\nHeidi Howard\nsensors and live painting\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nLiz Phillips\nis a New York–based artist and a pioneer of interactive sound art whose installations explore the relationships between sound\, technology\, architecture\, and the natural environment. For more than four decades\, she has created immersive works that use custom electronics\, sensors\, and spatial audio to transform audiences into active participants. Inspired by her early experiences in nature along the Hudson River\, Phillips has developed environments in which sound responds to human presence\, redefining the experience of space through interaction. Her work has been exhibited internationally at institutions including the Whitney Museum of American Art\, the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art\, the Walker Art Center\, Ars Electronica\, The Kitchen\, Creative Time\, and the Spoleto Festival USA. She has also collaborated with the Merce Cunningham Dance Company. Phillips is the recipient of a 2024 Anonymous Was A Woman Award and a United States Artists Fellowship\, recognizing her pioneering contributions to sound art and experimental media.\nHeidi Howard\nis a Queens\, New York–based artist whose practice spans portrait painting\, site-specific wall paintings\, sculpture\, and collaborative installations. Working across painting and spatial environments\, Howard explores the relationship between people and their surroundings\, often creating portraits with the sitter present. Their work has been exhibited at Wave Hill\, Gaa Gallery\, the Rhode Island School of Design\, the Southeastern Center for Contemporary Art\, the Queens Museum\, W139\, the Hunterdon Museum of Art\, and James Cohan Gallery. Howard has participated in residencies including the Terra Summer Residency\, Rauschenberg Residency\, Carrizozo AIR\, and Palazzo Monti. Their work has been featured in\nVogue\n\,\nThe Paris Review\n\,\nThe Brooklyn Rail\n\, and other publications. Howard’s first monograph\,\nColors Make Us Do Vibrant Deeds!\n\, was published in conjunction with a solo exhibition at the LeRoy Neiman Center for Print Studies at Columbia University.\nSanna Almajedi\n(b. 1993\, Baghdad) is a curator and writer based in New York\, currently serving as Performance Curator at e-flux and co-curator of the Immigrant Artist Biennial. She recently served as Performance Curator for the 13th Seoul Mediacity Biennale\, Séance: Technology of the Spirit\, held between August and November 2025. Almajedi co-curated Publishing Against the Grain–which was produced by Independent Curators International and toured institutions including Zeitz MOCAA and CCA\, Lagos–and more recently curated Babel at SARA’S / Dunkunsthalle\, Summer Dinner at Slip House\, and Bricks of Memory\, Fragments of Home\, an online exhibition for White Columns.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261029T000000Z
SUMMARY:Live Processing Paint and Sound: Liz Phillips and Heidi Howard
UID:a357ae4b-0204-4f2b-8782-28208984b7fa@a357.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/live-processing-paint-sound-liz-phillips-heidi-howard/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nA new live solo performance emerging from the world of the COIN COIN project moves between memory\, imagination\, and lived experience.\n\nEmerging from the world of their ongoing\nCOIN COIN\nproject\, composer\, saxophonist\, and sound experimentalist\nMatana Roberts\npresents a new live solo performance piece that moves between memory\, imagination\, and lived experience. Part story\, part call\, the work follows association rather than chronology with language\, gesture\, silence\, humor\, and chance shaping each telling.\nPart of the lineage of the\nCOIN COIN\nproject was conceived in its inception at Roulette. This work sits at its tail end as a celebratory loving postscript: an underbelly\, making space for ideas and questions that lie beyond the scope of the recordings.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nMatana Roberts\n(they/them) is a dynamic instrumentalist and multidisciplinary artist whose practice explores the mystical roots and intuitive\, spirit-raising traditions of American creative expression. Their innovative work has forged new conceptual approaches to considering narrativity\, history\, and political expression within improvisatory structures. More information is available at\nmatanaroberts.com\n.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261112T010000Z
SUMMARY:Matana Roberts Presents: “off the record…”
UID:a9222b67-e458-4ee8-aeb4-47ff18150239@a922.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/matana-roberts-presents-off-the-record/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nChoreographer explores nonsense-making through fragmentation\, the compression of sound\, and movement scores for her Roulette Commission.\n\nJessica Cook shares new material developed as part of her Roulette Commission.\nThe solo\nBreaks\nby Jessica Cook is a physical exploration of nonsense-making through fragmentation\, the compression of sound\, and movement scores. The work uses improvisation scores that splice\, isolate\, reference\, and congeal macro and micro textures\, paired with constructed sound costumes and “toys” created by the artist.\nJessica Cook\nperformer & choreographer\nAdditional performers TBA\n*FREE FOR ALL ROULETTE MEMBERS *\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nJessica Cook\n(b. Kingston\, NY) has worked as a Brooklyn based choreographer for over two decades. She combines movement\, the physicality of sound making\, and sculpture in creating absurdist and strained worlds. She has presented work in venues such as La MaMa Moves Festival\, LMCC’s River to River Festival\, Pageant\, Roulette Intermedium\, Movement Research at Judson Memorial Church\, and others.\nAs a dance artist she is currently working with Tess Dworman (\nThe Con\n)\, Benjamin Akio Kimmitch (\nAllegra\n)\, visual artist Amy Beecher\, Sam Kim\, and director/dreamworker Amanda Lovejoy Street.\nShe has been an artist-in-residence at The Chocolate Factory Theater\, Movement Research\, NY Live Arts Fresh Tracks\, and MoMA PS1. She is a 2005 graduate of the SUNY Purchase Dance Conservatory and Pacific College of Health and Science.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261121T010000Z
SUMMARY:Breaks: A Solo Performance by Jessica Cook (Night 2)
UID:8f2ec12a-0414-4e8a-bb87-d9b19e2c5f1e@8f2e.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/jessica-cook-night-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Triple Canopy’s annual symposium is a manifestation of the magazine’s concerns and collaborations\, in person and in real time. The multiday gathering brings together artists\, writers\, performers\, and researchers to address the role of culture in responding (or contributing) to a range of critical issues. The full lineup will be announced soon. RSVP to stay updated.\nThe 2026 Triple Canopy Symposium is supported by Critical Minded and will be hosted in multiple venues with additional dates presented in partnership with Public Records’s Durations festival.\nmore info\nHERE.\nTriple Canopy\nis a New York-based magazine that works with artists and writers to address the critical issues that define contemporary life.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261114T010000Z
SUMMARY:Triple Canopy Symposium 2026 (Day 1)
UID:f421e678-d3e5-4a4e-bf4b-c18324b1471b@f421.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/triple-canopy-symposium-2026-day-1/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nJazz Generations Initiative Continuum Fellow presents a three night residency featuring poets\, musicians\, and artists across generations.\n\n“The artists represented tonight I’ve admired for a long time. I’ve dreamed of a bill just focusing on their solo work and it’s finally happening. Playing solo is no easy feat: There’s nothing to fall back on\, and it’s the most intimate musical experience — direct communication with the audience.” — James Brandon Lewis\nAward-winning saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, a\nJazz Generations Initiative (JGI)\nContinuum Fellow\n\, presents a three-night residency at Roulette as part of his 2026 fellowship. The series brings together improvisers\, poets\, musicians\, and multidisciplinary artists from across media and generations.\nThis evening’s performance will feature a series of solo sets by\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, flutist/composer\nNicole Mitchell\n\, cellist/vocalist\nJanel Leppin\n\, singer/producer\nJenna Camille\n\, trombonist/composer\nKalia Vandever\n\, Grammy-winning sound chemist\nVal Jeanty\,\ntransdisciplinary artist\nShara Lunon\,\nand pianist/composer\nAlexis Marcelo\n.\nThe night will conclude with a collective improvisation featuring all of the artists.\n“A Saxophonist Who Embodies and Transcends Tradition” — The NY Times\n“James Brandon Lewis does not take the easy road. Having forged a singular sound on the tenor saxophone\, he could simply devise settings that showcase his brawny tone. Instead\, he has rooted his recent music in extramusical research.” — DownBeat\nPresented by the Jazz Generations Initiative in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nThe JGI Continuum Fellowship\nconvenes a cohort of New York-based early-to mid-career musicians and multidisciplinary artists to curate concerts and programs that foster creative exchange across artistic disciplines and generations.\nTenor saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\nhas emerged as a leading figure on the improvised music scene in New York City and across the world. The winner of seven DownBeat magazine awards (including “artist of the year” in the 2025 critics’ poll)\, and a 2026 Guggenheim Fellowship\, Lewis has led ensembles on some of the most prominent stages across the world\, from the Village Vanguard to the Bimhuis Muziekgebouw. He has received accolades from The New York Times\, NPR Music and countless other outlets.The late Sonny Rollins once told Lewis\, “When I listen to you\, I listen to Buddha\, I listen to Confucius… I listen to the deeper meaning of life. You are keeping the world in balance.” Lewis relocated to New York City in 2012\, and since then he has become known as one of the leading figures on the scene\, uniting his background in gospel and jazz with an interest in Western classical music\, hip-hop\, punk rock\, other music traditions from around the globe\, and art forms outside of music. His celebrated projects include the James Brandon Lewis Trio\, the American Book Award-winning Heroes Are Gang Leaders\, the Red Lily Quintet\, and his ongoing collaboration with the Messthetics\, which has yielded two critically acclaimed albums on Impulse! Records. Lewis\, who recently earned a PhD in Creativity from Rowan University in New Jersey\, also works across mediums as a writer and poet.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261016T000000Z
SUMMARY:Art of Solo: James Brandon Lewis\, Nicole Mitchell\, Janel Leppin\, Jenna Camille\, Kalia Vandever\, Val Jeanty\, Shara Lunon\, and Alexis Marcelo
UID:85c3adff-4f16-4534-ba9e-de68294c89f2@85c3.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/james-brandon-lewis-art-of-solo/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nJazz Generations Initiative Continuum Fellow presents a three-night residency featuring poets\, musicians\, and artists across generations.\n\n“I’ve always dreamed of having a night of music of various genres that bring us together rather than separate us. The world is full of sound and we should immerse ourselves in our shared experiences rather than difference. That experience is the love of sound.”\n—James Brandon Lewis\nAward-winning saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\n\, a\nJazz Generations Initiative (JGI)\nContinuum Fellow\n\, presents a three-night residency at Roulette as part of his 2026 fellowship. The series brings together improvisers\, poets\, musicians\, and multidisciplinary artists from across media and generations.\nThe series kicks off with a diverse night of music\, featuring the\nJames Brandon Lewis Trio\n\, the\nTrap Music Orchestra\n\,\nAngelica Sanchez\,\nand\nSheela Bringi and Clinton Patterson.\n“A Saxophonist Who Embodies and Transcends Tradition” — The NY Times\n“James Brandon Lewis does not take the easy road. Having forged a singular sound on the tenor saxophone\, he could simply devise settings that showcase his brawny tone. Instead\, he has rooted his recent music in extramusical research.” — DownBeat\nPresented by the Jazz Generations Initiative in partnership with Roulette.\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nThe JGI Continuum Fellowship\nconvenes a cohort of New York-based early-to mid-career musicians and multidisciplinary artists to curate concerts and programs that foster creative exchange across artistic disciplines and generations.\nTenor saxophonist and composer\nJames Brandon Lewis\nhas emerged as a leading figure on the improvised music scene in New York City and across the world. The winner of seven DownBeat magazine awards (including “artist of the year” in the 2025 critics’ poll)\, and a 2026 Guggenheim Fellowship\, Lewis has led ensembles on some of the most prominent stages across the world\, from the Village Vanguard to the Bimhuis Muziekgebouw. He has received accolades from The New York Times\, NPR Music and countless other outlets. The late Sonny Rollins once told Lewis\, “When I listen to you\, I listen to Buddha\, I listen to Confucius… I listen to the deeper meaning of life. You are keeping the world in balance.” Lewis relocated to New York City in 2012\, and since then he has become known as one of the leading figures on the scene\, uniting his background in gospel and jazz with an interest in Western classical music\, hip-hop\, punk rock\, other music traditions from around the globe\, and art forms outside of music. His celebrated projects include the James Brandon Lewis Trio\, the American Book Award-winning Heroes Are Gang Leaders\, the Red Lily Quintet\, and his ongoing collaboration with the Messthetics\, which has yielded two critically acclaimed albums on Impulse! Records. Lewis\, who recently earned a PhD in Creativity from Rowan University in New Jersey\, also works across mediums as a writer and poet.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260930T000000Z
SUMMARY:Bathed in Sound: James Brandon Lewis Trio // Trap Music Orchestra // Angelica Sanchez // Sheela Bringi & Clinton Patterson
UID:1e3c94a1-7998-42d6-ac69-601ba5ac6874@1e3c.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/james-brandon-lewis-continuum/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $25\nPurchase Tickets\nScholars of experimental notation discuss their publication The Scores Project\, followed by performances from members of The Rise of the Novel.\n\nEminent scholars of experimental music present a panel celebrating the publication of\nThe Scores Project: Experimental Notation in Music\, Art\, Poetry\, and Dance\n(Getty Publications\, 2025)\, followed by a Q&A and performances of experimental scores by members of\nThe Rise of the Novel\n.\nPanelists:\nGreg Albers\, Julia Bryan-Wilson\, Emily Ruth Capper\, Michael Gallope\, Natilee Harren\, John Hicks\, George E. Lewis\, Nancy Perloff\, and Benjamin Piekut.\nPerformers:\nThe Rise of the Novel (Travis Laplante\, Nate Wooley\, more to be announced)\nThe Scores Project\nis a unique digital publication that provides a comprehensive view of the global explosion of experimental scores during the 1960s\, alongside a complete edition of the groundbreaking\nAn Anthology of Chance Operations\n(1963). Featuring scores by John Cage\, George Brecht\, Sylvano Bussotti\, Morton Feldman\, Allan Kaprow\, Alison Knowles\, Jackson Mac Low\, Benjamin Patterson\, Yvonne Rainer\, Mieko Shiomi\, David Tudor\, and La Monte Young\, the publication includes expert commentaries\, audiovisual recordings of score performances\, and a digitized archive of over 2\,000 ephemera and audiovisual materials.\n“Gifting us visuals of rare notations\, press clippings\, recordings\, and photographs\, The Scores Project invites the reader into the manifold encounters that characterize the aftermath of any given performance.”—Nicole Kaack\, The Brooklyn Rail\n“The Scores Project is an essential research tool for anyone interested in the intersection of musical notation\, art\, poetry\, and dance from 1950 to 1975.” —Hannah B. Higgins\, Professor of Art\, University of Illinois Chicago\, and author of Fluxus Experience (2002) and The Grid Book (2009)\nA livestream will be available free of charge at 8pm on the day of the performance and archived for future viewing.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nJulia Bryan-Wilson\nis a professor of art history and gender studies at Columbia University. Her most recent book is\nLouise Nevelson’s Sculpture: Drag\, Color\, Join\, Face\n(Yale University Press\, 2023).\nEmily Ruth Capper\nis assistant professor in the Department of Art History at the University of Minnesota. Her first book\,\nHappening Pedagogy: Allan Kaprow’s Experiments in Instruction\, 1948–1968\n\, is forthcoming from the University of Chicago Press.\nMichael Gallope\nis professor of cultural studies and comparative literature at the University of Minnesota. His most recent book is\nThe Musician as Philosopher: New York’s Vernacular Avant-Garde\, 1958–1978\n(University of Chicago Press\, 2024).\nNatilee Harren\nis associate professor of modern and contemporary art history and theory at the University of Houston School of Art and the author of\nFluxus Forms: Scores\, Multiples\, and the Eternal Network\n(University of Chicago Press\, 2020).\nJohn Hicks\nis a lecturer in the Department of Cultural Studies and Comparative Literature at the University of Minnesota.\nGeorge E. Lewis\nis the Edwin H. Case Professor of American Music at Columbia University. He is the author of\nA Power Stronger Than Itself: The AACM and American Experimental Music\n(University of Chicago Press\, 2008).\nNancy Perloff\nis a curator at the Getty Research Institute.\nBenjamin Piekut\nis professor and chair of the Department of Music at Cornell University.\nThe Rise of the Novel\nis a new ensemble dedicated to prioritizing the spontaneity of music. The group celebrates the mixture of improvisation with composition—whether that comes from jazz or indeterminacy—preparing and performing it with the same respect and rigor normally reserved for concert hall modernism.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20260925T000000Z
SUMMARY:The Scores Project: A Book Release Show w/ The Rise of the Novel
UID:7a617c04-64b2-4869-97f3-9a4b84deec10@7a61.org
URL:https://roulette.org/event/the-scores-project-the-rise-of-the-novel/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Tickets $35\nPurchase Tickets\n"Queen of the Tar" performs solo and with ensemble for an evening that explores the rich traditions of Persian music.\n\nAcclaimed tar virtuoso Sahba Motallebi\, known as the “Queen of the Tar\,” opens the evening with an extraordinary solo performance. She’ll then be joined by an outstanding ensemble featuring\nMarjan Vahdat\n\, an award-winning vocalist celebrated for her distinctive contemporary interpretation of Persian music\;\nReza Mohsenipour\n\, a tar and bass tar master\, musicologist and composer whose work is devoted to preserving and expanding the rich traditions of Persian classical music\; and\nHamid Mohsenipour\n\, a dynamic percussionist\, flutist\, composer\, and co-founder of the Barbad Project with his brothers Reza and Navid.\n“A virtuoso performer.” – LA Weekly\n“There is no rock guitarist\, no oud player\, possibly no musician anywhere in the world with such subtle yet fearsome chops on a fretted instrument.” – New York Music Daily\nPresented by Robert Browning Associates and Lotus Music & Dance in partnership with Roulette.\nUSE CODE EARLYBIRDER AT CHECKOUT FOR $5 OFF TICKETS — ENDS AUGUST 31ST.\nSahba Motallebi\nis recognized internationally for her exquisite performances of Persian music on the tar\, a lute-like stringed instrument central to one of the world’s great musical traditions.  Widely recognized as a master of the Persian tar\, she has dedicated her life to preserving the rich traditions of Persian classical music while expanding its expressive possibilities through original composition and interdisciplinary artistic work. With a career that spans decades\, she has been acclaimed for her solo performances and collaborations with such renowned artists as Hossein Alizadeh\, Mohammad Reza Shajarian\, Yo-Yo Ma\, and Kayhan Kalhor\, and her work on the Grammy-winning\nFandango at the Wall\nalbum.
DURATION:PT2H
LOCATION:Roulette Intermedium\, 509 Atlantic Ave\, Brooklyn\, NY
DTSTART:20261106T010000Z
SUMMARY:Sahba Motallebi & Ensemble: Persian Music – an Evening of Solidarity and Harmony (RBA)
